WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.460
- Learn how to create and deploy

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.410
a full stack web application

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.700
using Svelte, Vercel, and Postgres,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.430
and they'll do it all on your browser.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.480
- Hello and welcome to the
Full Stack Development Course.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.930
My name is Mike Nikles.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.960
I am your instructor for this course,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.020
and I will talk about
myself and my background

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.810
in just a little bit.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.020
Before we get to that,

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.380
I want to introduce you to the
course, the technology stack,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.330
and a little bit of how we are
going to be developing this.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.910
Now, why do we do a full
stack web development course?

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.320
I've been in the industry
for a couple of years.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.690
And one thing I noticed is that
there's a lot of information

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.390
online about web development,
different frameworks,

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.180
different technology stacks.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.730
There's a lot of information
about backend development,

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.610
and there's a lot of
information about how to build

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.150
and model your database
and your backend systems.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:00.577
Now, what I think has been
missing is this entire end-to-end

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.740
full stack web development, like what,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.760
how do you connect the
website to the backend?

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.430
Like of course there's
REST APIs, there's GraphQL.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.070
There's different ways of communicating

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.160
between the frontend and the backend,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.830
you can find that information online.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.240
It's again, like anything
else, a lot of stuff out there,

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.150
but how do you really connect
the front end to the backend?

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.600
What are some of the best practices,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.840
some gotchas to watch out for?

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.190
And the same is true for
the connection between

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.500
the server side or the backend
and your database, right?

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:43.260
You could use raw SQL queries,
you could use an object

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.260
relational mapping, an ORM,
you could use a NoSQL database

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.820
where you persist JSON-like
data or documents.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.650
But again, how exactly
are you going to do that?

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:57.990
And in this course,

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.370
I am going to teach
you the full end to end

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.050
from building the web application,
connecting it to an API,

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.120
and then persisting data in
the backend in a database.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.210
We will be talking about the entire thing,

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.320
we will also be deploying that

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.550
into a production-like environment,

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.010
so that at the end of the course,

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.950
you have what it really takes
to start your next project.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.210
Whatever you are going to
build by the end of this course

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.870
will be the starting point
for your next project.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.231
So you don't have to build all this,

00:02:30.231 --> 00:02:31.631
gluing together by yourself.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:36.430
Let's talk about where we
are going to develop that.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:37.820
Most of you are very familiar

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.230
with your local development environment,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.220
where you have a bunch of
tools installed, a terminal,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.430
probably, some kind of
command line interface,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.790
and probably some editor
of one sort or another.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.610
In this course,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.290
I'm going to be using
something called Gitpod.io,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:01.910
Gitpod is providing developer environments

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:05.270
in the cloud for each task
that we're going to work on.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.360
So imagine instead of, you know,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.140
curating and maintaining
our local environment,

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.700
where we install certain versions

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.700
of Node or Java, what we
get is a new environment,

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.800
always cleaned from scratch,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.710
automatically set up for each
task we're going to work on.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.150
We will get into that in
more detail as the course

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.600
progresses, and I will
introduce this as we go.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.180
One thing to keep in
mind is if you do prefer

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.430
your local environment,
that's perfectly fine.

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.742
You can follow along with everything we do

00:03:37.742 --> 00:03:40.130
by using your local environment.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.580
There's no problem with that.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.130
Let's talk about the technologies.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:47.220
You can see on the screen, on the website,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.940
we will be using a
framework called Svelte.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.120
Svelte is currently in version three.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.610
It's been stable for a while,
and it's used in production

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:59.610
by a number of big companies,
the API slash backend,

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.970
and also the hosting a
framework for Svelte itself

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.950
will be SvelteKit.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.560
SvelteKit as you can tell by the name

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.540
is developed by the same team.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.910
And very recently, the
founder and creator of Svelte,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:19.710
Rich Harris announced that he
is joining Vercel full-time

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:24.440
so Svelte is now backed by a
large player in the industry

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.950
who really cares about
making the web faster.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.970
Lastly, we need some kind of database.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.450
In this course, we're
going to use Postgres.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:37.580
We will be talking about other
databases at the very end

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:41.020
when we talk about next
steps, and you know,

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.530
this is a stack that I chose

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.770
because I'm very familiar with it,

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:46.310
and I think it is future proof in terms

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:50.900
of what you learned and what
you can apply at your work

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.980
or in your next, you
know, free time project.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:55.340
over the next few years.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:00.780
Lastly, we will be using
two deployment platforms,

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.330
one is Vercel, we will deploy
the SvelteKit application

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:09.060
to ourself and serve our
web application from there,

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.710
and secondly, we really use
something called Railway,

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:16.200
a hosting provider where
we will be hosting Postgres

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:20.550
and you'll see how easy it is
to set up a Postgres database

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:24.470
on Railway connect with
SvelteKit in Vercel

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.170
and get everything up and running.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.690
That is a very high level
overview of what are we going to

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:34.070
be talking about and how are
we going to be developing this.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.400
Let me also give you a
little bit of background

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.160
about myself so that you have an idea

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:40.410
of what I'm talking about

00:05:41.338 --> 00:05:44.080
and why I do have the
experience to teach this course

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.180
and help you with your next
full-stack application.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.080
So I have been a web developer since 2000,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.840
so it's been a couple of
years and the web has gone

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.260
through a number of transitions.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.130
You know, things like jQuery.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:00.900
Well, first of all, PHP
and jQuery came out,

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.600
a whole bunch of different
things happened since then.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.200
React is very big, lots
of people using that.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.507
And then I started using Svelte

00:06:10.507 --> 00:06:13.920
about two and a half years ago
when it was at version two.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.930
And I've advocated for it
ever since, and, you know,

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.600
with the recent announcement
of Vercel backing Svelte,

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.830
I think this is a really strong framework

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.670
to use for a course like that.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.890
Now at the moment, I'm a
developer advocate at Gitpod,

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.290
and that's obviously why
we will be using Gitpod

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.990
for this course, and I will
be introducing the concept,

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:37.830
as I mentioned before, it's not mandatory

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.450
to use it, but it is
there to give you an idea

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.700
of what the future of
development looks like.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.200
You can find me on my
blog at mikeNikles.com.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.570
This is where I share, you know,

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:54.490
any of the open source
project I launch or release.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:55.740
You can go there and have a look.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.330
There's a bunch of Svelte related content.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:01.620
There is React related
content from the past.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.030
So go check that out

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.020
