you get that out of the box

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.010
if you create a new Svelte application

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:06.290
that uses the default template.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:09.910
Now, why are we copying
something that already exists?

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.730
Because this course is about
full stack web development.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.700
So I don't want to spend
time building the UI,

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:19.520
and instead we will use something existing

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:22.590
and we'll spend the time
in the course, focusing

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:26.820
on deploying, gluing the things together,

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.180
writing scripts and automating
a lot of the process

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:32.630
for the deployment of the
full stack application.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:35.110
What did you want to talk about

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:38.940
though focusing on the why
we chose a todo application.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:42.950
And the reason for that is very simple.

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:46.170
A todo application gives
us everything you need

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:48.550
in order to build your own project

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:51.020
once you're done with this course.

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:55.120
To get started, we have
to create todo-items.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:58.600
So we can tap here to add a new item.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:03.070
They will teach us how to
send new data to the backend

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:05.910
from where we will persist
it in the database.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:07.740
You'll always need that functionality,

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:09.590
no matter what application you build.

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:12.573
Secondly we will learn how to read

00:15:12.573 --> 00:15:15.670
the items that we created.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:18.550
So that teaches kind of the opposite way.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:20.800
We're going to go to the database

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:23.500
and ask for information that
will be sent to the backend,

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:26.600
and from there to our
front end application.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:30.750
Next, we will learn how
to update an existing item

00:15:31.727 --> 00:15:33.660
by checking it off or marking it as done.

00:15:34.614 --> 00:15:36.100
That will teach us how to

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:39.840
go to the database and edit
or update an existing item

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.580
that's already persisted.

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:44.190
And lastly, we will learn over here,

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:48.240
how we delete an item from the database.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:49.890
If you take the initial letters

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:52.730
of create, read, update, and delete.

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:55.350
You, get the acronym CRUD
that you may have heard of

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:59.500
or read about before CRUD is
the foundation that you need

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:02.850
for any project that you
will be working on in future.

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:05.320
So if you know how to do that as part

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:07.810
of a very simple todo application,

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:10.900
you can apply that exact same concept to

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:14.540
your next billion dollar idea
that you will be developing.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:17.510
With that out of the way,

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:21.240
I want to move on and
give you an introduction

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:25.520
to the architecture of the
entire end-to-end stack

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:28.160
so that we can see how the front end,

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.230
the API and the database all
communicate with each other.

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:38.280
Now that we've seen the UI
of the todo application,

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:41.770
I want to talk about the
end-to-end architecture.

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:45.580
What is this going to look
like once it's all deployed

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:47.868
onto Vercel and Railway?

00:16:47.868 --> 00:16:50.870
The very first thing we will be working

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:53.320
on is the front end, we
were going to build that UI.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:56.380
It will not do anything when
we click on any buttons,

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:59.600
but at least we will
build the user interface

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:01.560
with all the different
states and all that,

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:06.220
so that the real focus after
that can be on the rest of

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:09.350
the architecture, all the stuff
that's white at the moment.

00:17:09.350 --> 00:17:12.350
So let's have a look at
what we are going to build.

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:14.300
As I mentioned, we will be using Svelte

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:18.710
for the front end to develop
that web application.

00:17:18.710 --> 00:17:22.220
Now, in order to serve the web application

00:17:23.212 --> 00:17:25.560
to our users, we have
to host it somewhere.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:29.110
And the hosting for the
web application will happen

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:32.530
on Vercel and we will be using SvelteKit.

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:36.070
They have to same logo, but
Svelte on the front end,

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:39.900
SvelteKit on Vercel to
host the application.

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:45.690
Also in Vercel, in SvelteKit on Vercel,

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:50.240
we will have the APIs
exposed for the front end.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:52.270
So if we look at that,

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:57.270
we will have the frontend make
API requests to a REST API

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:02.110
hosted on Vercel, in
SvelteKit for example,

00:18:02.110 --> 00:18:04.560
to fetch all todos.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.810
So a get request slash todos

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:13.300
and then the SvelteKit API
will return a JSON object

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:17.520
with the list of the todo-items
that you can see in the UI.

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:19.270
We saw that just a few minutes ago.

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:24.950
In order for SvelteKit
to return that JSON,

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:27.720
we need to fetch it from the database.

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:30.370
So let's have a look
at how this looks like.

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:35.370
Somewhere we'll have to
write select star from todos,

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:38.770
sent that query to the Postgres database

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:40.750
that is deployed on Railway.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:46.290
The Postgres database will
return the SQL response

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:49.030
that contains the rows from the database

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:52.310
for all these items that are persisted.

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:56.170
And then it will be sent to SvelteKit

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:58.930
and returned to the Svelte application

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:01.370
in the browser, from
where we can then render

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:04.990
the JSON and display this beautiful list.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:07.180
You notice there's a missing piece.

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:11.402
And that missing piece is Prisma,

00:19:11.402 --> 00:19:14.010
a library we are going to be using

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:19.010
that helps us translate from the requests

00:19:19.472 --> 00:19:21.480
that we get in get todos,

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:25.170
we will be able to just
call a few functions,

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:28.030
telling Prisma to go get our todos

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:30.860
and Prisma will take
care of writing the SQL

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:35.180
or generating the SQL that
will be sent to Postgres.

00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:38.365
Equally, when the response

00:19:38.365 --> 00:19:41.230
from Postgres comes back onto Vercel,

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:44.350
Prisma will be the first point of contact

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:47.180
to take that raw SQL response,

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.680
convert it into JavaScript objects

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:54.230
that we can then turn into JSON responses

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:57.460
that are being sent back to the browser.

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:59.930
So Svelte on the front end,

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:04.220
sending an API request to
SvelteKit from where we use

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:08.670
Prisma in order to send a
request to the Postgres database.

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:11.720
On the way back when
the response comes back,

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:15.590
Prisma converts the SQL
response to JavaScript objects,

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:17.551
SvelteKit creates the response

00:20:17.551 --> 00:20:20.060
for the browser, sends it back,

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:24.190
and the browser displays the
todo-items that we fetched

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:29.190
in the first place, a very
standard three tier application.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:33.170
And this is exactly what we're
going to build as part of

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:37.930
this course, so let's move on
and get started with coding.

