00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:47.380
you can always look in the footer

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:50.900
where you have a branch
name with your user name

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:55.900
slash the name of the issue
dash the number of the issue.

00:35:57.220 --> 00:35:59.820
So if we look at the issue
here, it is initialized

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:04.820
the application number one,
the branch name is the same.

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:07.320
Now you remember,

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:10.290
I never actually created a new Git branch.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:15.720
All I did was I started to
Gitpod developer environment

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:18.600
from that issue by
clicking the Gitpod button.

00:36:19.600 --> 00:36:21.980
So Gitpod is all about
automating your developer

00:36:21.980 --> 00:36:24.130
environment and making
you more productive.

00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:28.870
Now it knows that in 99% of the time,

00:36:28.870 --> 00:36:32.650
when you open a Gitpod developer
environment from an issue,

00:36:33.550 --> 00:36:36.770
you're probably going
to work on that feature

00:36:36.770 --> 00:36:39.220
and you want to eventually
create a pull request

00:36:39.220 --> 00:36:40.970
to merge back into the main branch.

00:36:41.970 --> 00:36:44.560
So GitPod is smart enough to just create

00:36:44.560 --> 00:36:48.770
that branch for you with a
sensible default, in this case,

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:52.470
it's user name slash issue
dot title dash a number.

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:55.990
We don't have to do that
any time by ourselves.

00:36:55.990 --> 00:36:57.900
It's just done for us.

00:36:57.900 --> 00:36:58.740
So if you don't remember

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:01.440
what issue number you're
working on, check the footer.

00:37:02.360 --> 00:37:06.520
Next up, we can create the pull request.

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:10.900
And it's telling us that
there's no upstream branch

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:13.450
for that name, that's fine.

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:16.430
Please publish it for us automatically.

00:37:16.430 --> 00:37:20.410
We can say publish branch
that pushes the branch,

00:37:20.410 --> 00:37:23.770
creates a pull request in GitHub.

00:37:23.770 --> 00:37:26.610
And you can see now we have a preview here

00:37:26.610 --> 00:37:28.880
of what that PR looks like,

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:31.040
and we could even merge
it right away from here.

00:37:32.260 --> 00:37:34.340
What I want to do instead is go back

00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:37.260
to GitHub and show you what it did for us.

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:39.990
It created a pull request,
it linked it to our issue

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:43.010
number one, because I said
close issue number one.

00:37:43.010 --> 00:37:47.510
If you go to the PR, see
this closed issue number one.

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:51.970
That's how GitHub knew
that they are connected.

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:56.410
And usually we would ask a
colleague to go and review that.

00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:59.630
We will do that by ourselves
in just a little bit.

00:37:59.630 --> 00:38:02.690
For now, we can safely go in here and say

00:38:02.690 --> 00:38:05.590
that we would like to
merge that pull request.

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:08.450
So we say merge, and we confirm.

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:15.680
Now that merged the PR,
you can see it's merged.

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:18.280
We don't need the branch anymore.

00:38:18.280 --> 00:38:21.942
And if we go back to
issues our issue got closed

00:38:21.942 --> 00:38:25.400
because the application
is now initialized.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:30.420
We still have that
environment running here.

00:38:30.420 --> 00:38:33.760
And what do we want to do with that?

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:37.610
If you work locally, then you
would keep that environment,

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:39.910
you would switch back to the main branch,

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:42.200
pull down the latest
changes we just merged.

00:38:43.100 --> 00:38:45.310
And then when you work on the next issue,

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:49.210
you would create a new
issue, create a new branch.

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:50.880
You know, start working
and do that over again.

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:54.570
With Gitpod because we
get a new environment

00:38:54.570 --> 00:38:55.910
for every task we work on,

00:38:56.850 --> 00:38:59.310
we can either just close the browser tab

00:38:59.310 --> 00:39:02.400
and that environment will
automatically shut down

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:03.400
a few minutes later.

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:07.460
Or we can explicitly go
to the hamburger menu

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:11.050
and say, Gitpod, stop workspace.

00:39:12.190 --> 00:39:14.300
And then it starts to shut down

00:39:14.300 --> 00:39:16.200
and it will eventually disappear.

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:20.460
We no longer need that, we
never have to come back to it.

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:23.489
If for some reason you

00:39:23.489 --> 00:39:26.770
do want to get back to an old environment,

00:39:26.770 --> 00:39:30.990
you can always go to Gitpod.io/workspaces,

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:35.000
and you will get access to
that or click open workspace

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:38.130
from this tab, which would
open it again if you needed to.

00:39:38.130 --> 00:39:42.900
The dashboard will show you a
list of existing workspaces.

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:45.550
As I said, there's very few reasons

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:47.240
where you really want to go back.

00:39:48.320 --> 00:39:51.700
We are done with that, so
we close the browser tab.

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:53.650
We don't need the documentation either.

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:54.483
Goodbye.

