00:41:23.500 --> 00:41:26.330
on Gitpod, straight on the main branch.

00:41:27.320 --> 00:41:30.540
And use to walk you through

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:33.620
and explain the different
files and directories.

00:41:35.513 --> 00:41:38.480
Okay, that should only
take another second.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:44.530
We don't need to get started
page, let's have a look, so

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:52.460
the arguably most important
file in this entire application

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:54.830
is the package.JSON file.

00:41:55.840 --> 00:41:57.690
If we open that up,

00:41:57.690 --> 00:42:01.390
you can see that it has a name of version

00:42:02.837 --> 00:42:04.160
and that's a good start,

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:06.060
you can start tweaking that as you go.

00:42:07.100 --> 00:42:09.990
Now, the other thing to
look at is the dependencies.

00:42:10.990 --> 00:42:12.920
The first thing you notice is that

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:16.290
it only has a dev dependencies.

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:20.050
So when you build a Svelte application

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:22.230
or in this case SvelteKit application,

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:28.070
then Svelte itself is
not really a framework

00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:32.800
that needs to be part of
your production dependencies.

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:35.090
In fact svelte is a compiler

00:42:36.130 --> 00:42:40.370
that converts or compiles our Svelte files

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:44.470
into pure JavaScript that can be deployed

00:42:44.470 --> 00:42:48.940
to the browser where the
browser then just parses that.

00:42:48.940 --> 00:42:51.820
So it's very little overhead.

00:42:51.820 --> 00:42:56.790
And the performance
therefore is quite good

00:42:56.790 --> 00:42:59.010
on the browser side,

00:42:59.010 --> 00:43:01.040
because there's a lot
less code to download,

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:04.230
a lot less JavaScript
to parse and execute.

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:09.230
So because of that, you notice
that the Svelte dependency,

00:43:11.710 --> 00:43:15.770
as well as the Svelte JS
slash kit dependency for our

00:43:15.770 --> 00:43:20.050
application framework, they
are both dev dependencies.

00:43:21.370 --> 00:43:25.070
The other dev dependencies
you notice is Svelte-check

00:43:25.070 --> 00:43:28.250
pre-process, we'll look at all that later.

00:43:29.410 --> 00:43:31.950
What we also want to
look at is the scripts.

00:43:31.950 --> 00:43:34.810
There are a number of interesting scripts

00:43:34.810 --> 00:43:37.990
that need to be talked
about, the first one is dev.

00:43:37.990 --> 00:43:40.250
If you're going to run that
out would be NPM run dev,

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:41.750
we saw that a few minutes ago,

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:45.890
it uses a binary called SvelteKit

00:43:45.890 --> 00:43:49.670
that's provided by our
SvelteJS/Kit dependency.

00:43:50.990 --> 00:43:53.960
SvelteKit takes a number of arguments.

00:43:53.960 --> 00:43:55.660
If you pass in dev,

00:43:55.660 --> 00:43:58.080
it starts a local development server

00:43:58.080 --> 00:44:01.640
that also provides hot module reloading.

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:03.680
So we can make code changes

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:05.920
and we see the changes in the browser

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:09.210
without the need to
refresh the application.

00:44:10.330 --> 00:44:13.950
The next script that's defined is build.

00:44:16.084 --> 00:44:17.940
Because Svelte is a compiler,

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:19.880
before we can deploy the application.

00:44:19.880 --> 00:44:22.540
We have to run the build script,

00:44:22.540 --> 00:44:25.710
which goes through our entire application,

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:27.630
compiles all the files

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:32.630
and generates an output into
a directory here as well,

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:35.650
the root, we will see that later.

00:44:37.900 --> 00:44:40.707
That directory will then
be consumed by adapters.,

00:44:41.680 --> 00:44:43.530
we will talk about that
when we get to deploy

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:47.943
the application, but basically
it's an intermediary format

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:53.910
of JavaScript files and
other files needed to deploy.

00:44:55.090 --> 00:44:56.510
So the build step
compiles the application,

00:44:56.510 --> 00:45:00.250
creates that output,
and then we'll skip on,

00:45:00.250 --> 00:45:01.400
and we look at preview.

00:45:04.006 --> 00:45:07.650
Preview allows us to start a local preview

00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:10.880
based on the generated
output from the build script.

00:45:10.880 --> 00:45:15.880
So if you run NPM run build,
and then NPM run preview,

00:45:16.560 --> 00:45:20.980
you get a preview of your
application the way it will look

00:45:20.980 --> 00:45:24.450
in production, so no hot module reloading,

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:27.080
no development server running.

00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:31.370
This is really much
more closely reflecting

00:45:31.370 --> 00:45:34.070
what the production environment
is going to look like.

00:45:35.710 --> 00:45:37.780
There's two more scripts I
want to quickly talk about,

00:45:37.780 --> 00:45:39.430
this is check.

00:45:39.430 --> 00:45:42.830
So check allows us to use a package

00:45:42.830 --> 00:45:44.470
or a binary called Svelte-check.

00:45:45.390 --> 00:45:49.360
This is very handy because
it's kind of like ESLingt,

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:53.480
which checks your JavaScript
code, Svelte-check

00:45:54.950 --> 00:45:58.310
reviews your Svelte files and
gives you a warning or hints

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:00.280
if certain things are missing.

00:46:00.280 --> 00:46:04.050
For example, if you have
accessibility issues

00:46:04.050 --> 00:46:08.880
in your website, such
as missing attributes

00:46:08.880 --> 00:46:12.290
for accessibility on
your images or buttons,

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:16.530
the Svelte-check binary
will tell us about that

00:46:16.530 --> 00:46:19.050
and give us the output in the console

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:21.650
so we can then address
it and make sure that the

00:46:21.650 --> 00:46:25.160
applications we build are
of the highest quality.

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:28.600
Check:watch is basically the same thing,

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:29.920
but it runs continuously.

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:33.170
So while we're making code changes,

00:46:33.170 --> 00:46:34.930
if we run that in the terminal,

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:38.510
it will continue to output
any potential errors,

00:46:38.510 --> 00:46:42.160
and we can fix that while we're
working on the application.

00:46:44.270 --> 00:46:49.090
The package script that we
skipped so far is really

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:52.040
something more advanced
that we don't really need

00:46:52.040 --> 00:46:53.390
to worry about right now.

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:56.530
But if you were to develop a library

00:46:56.530 --> 00:46:58.500
that you want to publish to NPM,

00:46:58.500 --> 00:47:00.398
you could use to packet script

00:47:00.398 --> 00:47:03.560
to help you with that process.

00:47:03.560 --> 00:47:07.730
For the time being, we can
safely ignore that and move on.

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:12.760
So that makes up the
content of the package JSON.

00:47:12.760 --> 00:47:17.320
The next thing that's important
is the Svelte config file.

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:19.950
If we look at that,

00:47:21.050 --> 00:47:24.180
you can see that there's a
couple of things going on.

00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:28.977
The first is the config
options that we export

00:47:31.370 --> 00:47:35.040
contain a pre-process property.

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:40.390
And this basically just informs

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:45.930
the Svelte compiler how
to prepare the code.

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:49.080
So if we were to use SASS

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:53.410
or other features in our CSS, for example,

00:47:53.410 --> 00:47:57.620
we could configure that here
and the Svelte compiler would

00:47:57.620 --> 00:48:01.720
take that into account
when it processes the code.

00:48:03.090 --> 00:48:05.660
The other important property is kit.

00:48:06.500 --> 00:48:09.300
And one thing you notice
is there's an adapter

00:48:09.300 --> 00:48:11.250
defined at the moment.

00:48:11.250 --> 00:48:15.830
This is using an auto
adapter provided by SvelteJS.

00:48:15.830 --> 00:48:20.830
So later we will replace
that with a Vercel adapter

00:48:21.090 --> 00:48:23.430
so that we can deploy to Vercel.

00:48:23.430 --> 00:48:27.480
If we were to deploy to
Netlify or CloudFlare workers,

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:30.560
we could install the appropriate adapter,

00:48:31.620 --> 00:48:34.980
replace that line, and
we would be good to go.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:37.290
We will do that later in the course.

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:42.780
The next thing is a target.

00:48:42.780 --> 00:48:44.710
And you can see the comment.

00:48:44.710 --> 00:48:48.450
This is telling the application

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:52.200
where to render the Svelte app.

00:48:52.200 --> 00:48:54.320
It's similar to what you
might be might have seen

00:48:54.320 --> 00:48:55.810
in other application frameworks,

00:48:55.810 --> 00:48:59.920
where you have a difficult
root, with an id root.

00:49:00.920 --> 00:49:03.720
So it is under source app HTML.

00:49:03.720 --> 00:49:06.990
There is a if with an ID Svelte,

00:49:06.990 --> 00:49:09.570
this is where the content
of our Svelte files

00:49:09.570 --> 00:49:10.670
will be rendered into.

00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:15.130
Let's have a look at
that src/app.html next.

00:49:16.290 --> 00:49:19.200
So source, app.html.

00:49:20.820 --> 00:49:25.180
This is the entry point
where we can specify HTML,

00:49:26.390 --> 00:49:28.550
all the attributes we need there.

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:30.410
We can specify the head, the body,

00:49:30.410 --> 00:49:35.410
anything that is shared
across the entire application.

00:49:35.630 --> 00:49:39.790
It's a good place to
define common meta tags

00:49:39.790 --> 00:49:43.140
that do not change like, you know,

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:48.140
the charset put in the favicon,
viewport things, all that.

00:49:48.730 --> 00:49:52.060
Later, we will learn how
we can populate the head

00:49:52.060 --> 00:49:55.680
with additional information,
for example, the page title,

00:49:55.680 --> 00:49:57.980
which changes depending
on the page we are on.

00:49:58.830 --> 00:49:59.663
And also down here,

00:49:59.663 --> 00:50:04.650
we see that div with id Svelte
where eventually the Svelte

00:50:04.650 --> 00:50:08.710
application will be rendered
into either server side,

00:50:08.710 --> 00:50:12.710
if we do a server side
rendering or on the client,

00:50:12.710 --> 00:50:15.770
when somebody navigates
from one page to another.

00:50:18.810 --> 00:50:23.200
Okay, the next important directory

00:50:23.200 --> 00:50:26.180
is under source and it's called routes.

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:27.500
And within that,

00:50:27.500 --> 00:50:29.990
we have a file currently
called index.Svelte.

00:50:31.510 --> 00:50:33.540
If we open that up,

00:50:33.540 --> 00:50:36.800
you can see there's an H1 element

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:41.800
with a welcome to SvelteKit
content, as well as a paragraph

00:50:42.030 --> 00:50:46.150
that says, visit some link
to read the documentation.

00:50:48.750 --> 00:50:53.340
That code is what's eventually added

00:50:53.340 --> 00:50:55.370
into that Svelte body here.

00:50:57.298 --> 00:51:00.600
So if we were to quickly
go to the hamburger menu,

00:51:00.600 --> 00:51:03.050
terminal open a new terminal.

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:06.700
And we run NPM run dev,

00:51:06.700 --> 00:51:08.900
remember that starts
our development server.

00:51:10.090 --> 00:51:12.210
And oh, of course, that doesn't work.

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:13.740
We have to first NPM install.

00:51:14.840 --> 00:51:18.010
We didn't have any dependencies installed.

00:51:20.180 --> 00:51:21.980
And with the dependencies installed,

00:51:23.090 --> 00:51:25.150
now we can run NPM run dev.

00:51:28.250 --> 00:51:32.630
And that spins up port 3000
Gitpod tells us about that.

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:34.380
We can preview, see, this is

00:51:36.100 --> 00:51:38.250
the code I was talking
about in the routes.

00:51:39.100 --> 00:51:40.250
So if we make this,

00:51:44.680 --> 00:51:49.650
we can see there's a div
ID Svelte H1 in paragraph,

00:51:49.650 --> 00:51:53.790
which maps to, what we have
here, H1 and paragraph.

00:51:56.520 --> 00:52:00.770
Alright, we will add some
syntax highlighting to that file

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:04.850
very shortly, for the time
being, the VS Code that we are

00:52:04.850 --> 00:52:09.170
using here, doesn't have the
Svelte extension installed,

00:52:09.170 --> 00:52:12.580
and that's why we don't see anything yet,

00:52:12.580 --> 00:52:15.030
but that's an easy fix
we'll do in a few minutes.

00:52:16.380 --> 00:52:18.370
Good, so let's get rid of this

00:52:19.830 --> 00:52:22.150
and close all these terminals.

00:52:24.380 --> 00:52:25.213
Good.

00:52:25.213 --> 00:52:27.220
The other thing I want to briefly touch on

00:52:29.753 --> 00:52:31.480
is the tsconfig.json.

00:52:32.530 --> 00:52:36.673
Because we select the
TypeScript as a framework

00:52:37.540 --> 00:52:40.630
that we're going to use
to build this application,

00:52:40.630 --> 00:52:42.480
SvelteKit automatically created

00:52:42.480 --> 00:52:44.150
that configuration file for us,

00:52:45.903 --> 00:52:49.840
and it has all the things we
need for this application.

00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:54.840
So there's no need to understand
each of these properties

00:52:55.280 --> 00:52:58.370
and configuration values,
if you are curious,

00:52:58.370 --> 00:53:02.420
the TypeScript documentation
contains notes about

00:53:02.420 --> 00:53:06.830
each one of those you can
mouse over and then just click

00:53:06.830 --> 00:53:09.710
the link to the docs, but as I said,

00:53:09.710 --> 00:53:13.750
there is no need to understand
what is going on here.

00:53:13.750 --> 00:53:18.750
It is safe to assume that
the Svelte developer team

00:53:19.080 --> 00:53:21.640
made a good decision here on the defaults.

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:23.370
So we can trust them,

00:53:23.370 --> 00:53:25.220
that they did the right thing for us.

00:53:26.950 --> 00:53:31.950
Good, that concludes the
introduction to the files.

00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:34.250
So you know, it's very basic for now.

00:53:34.250 --> 00:53:37.740
Really, all we need to know at
the moment is source routes,

00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:41.540
index is our index page in the URL.

00:53:41.540 --> 00:53:45.560
If we were to create a about us page

00:53:47.500 --> 00:53:51.860
or any other route, then Svelte
uses a file based routing.

00:53:51.860 --> 00:53:54.310
So if we do routes new file,

00:53:54.310 --> 00:53:56.760
and we say about-us.Svelte,

00:53:57.730 --> 00:54:02.000
and then here, we would
have an H1 called about us.

00:54:03.500 --> 00:54:06.380
And if we were to go and
start to dev server again

00:54:06.380 --> 00:54:10.960
through the terminal and NPM run dev,

00:54:15.150 --> 00:54:19.240
if we go back to the tab that
we had opened previously,

00:54:19.240 --> 00:54:23.420
we can now go to the URL
and do slash about-us.

00:54:24.910 --> 00:54:27.870
And then we see the content
of the about us page.

00:54:28.940 --> 00:54:31.550
So this is how you can
create multiple pages.

00:54:32.810 --> 00:54:35.070
In our case, all we're going to do is use

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:37.290
that index page to build our application,

00:54:37.290 --> 00:54:39.700
but it's good to know how you
can extend your application

00:54:39.700 --> 00:54:41.220
to add additional content.

00:54:43.280 --> 00:54:46.960
Fantastic, so this is
all we want to talk about

00:54:46.960 --> 00:54:48.500
for the time being.

00:54:48.500 --> 00:54:53.050
Let's now go on and
continue with the setup

00:54:53.050 --> 00:54:56.620
of our developer environment
and this environment here,

00:54:56.620 --> 00:54:59.920
we just use that for a showcase purposes.

00:54:59.920 --> 00:55:03.990
So even though I have changed
files here, I can just say,

00:55:03.990 --> 00:55:08.390
go to the menu here and
say, Gitpod, stop workspace.

00:55:08.390 --> 00:55:10.760
And I don't have to worry
about these changes.

00:55:10.760 --> 00:55:14.150
They will never interfere with
my new environment that I'm

00:55:14.150 --> 00:55:18.920
going to create to work on
as my part of the next task.

00:55:18.920 --> 00:55:21.390
So we did that, we can close the tab.

00:55:21.390 --> 00:55:23.800
We also don't need this anymore.

00:55:23.800 --> 00:55:27.390
And we are back to the GitHub repository.

00:55:27.390 --> 00:55:30.120
So with that, let's swiftly move on.

00:55:33.500 --> 00:55:36.290
The next section of this course

00:55:37.827 --> 00:55:39.830
is only for people who follow along

00:55:39.830 --> 00:55:43.870
using Gitpod, the automated
developer environment.

00:55:43.870 --> 00:55:47.030
So if you prefer to work
on your local computer

00:55:47.030 --> 00:55:51.540
and don't want to experience
the way it works on Gitpod,

00:55:51.540 --> 00:55:55.110
then feel free to jump
down to the description and

00:55:55.110 --> 00:55:56.880
click ahead to the next section.

00:55:58.800 --> 00:56:01.380
For anybody who does
want to work on Gitpod

00:56:01.380 --> 00:56:05.560
and do remember you get 50
hours for free every month.

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:08.010
So that's a good
opportunity to play around,

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:11.670
get an idea of how this
way of working helps you

00:56:11.670 --> 00:56:13.370
and makes you more productive.

00:56:14.810 --> 00:56:19.810
What are we going to do now
is configure Gitpod so that

00:56:19.900 --> 00:56:23.570
when we start a new developer
environment for this project,

00:56:24.490 --> 00:56:27.790
we want to Gitpod to run certain commands

00:56:27.790 --> 00:56:29.250
automatically for us.

00:56:29.250 --> 00:56:34.250
We also want to configure the
VS Code in order to make sure

00:56:35.960 --> 00:56:38.733
everybody who works on this project

00:56:38.733 --> 00:56:40.720
has the same VS Code extension,

00:56:40.720 --> 00:56:44.810
so everybody gets a great
experience out of the box

00:56:44.810 --> 00:56:47.290
and if they want to, they
can install other extensions

00:56:47.290 --> 00:56:49.110
that they personally prefer.

00:56:49.110 --> 00:56:53.110
But at least there's a
base set up that makes

00:56:53.110 --> 00:56:56.060
for a great experience for
everybody who uses that project.

00:56:57.220 --> 00:57:02.220
So let's go ahead and configure
the Gitpod environment.

00:57:04.370 --> 00:57:07.570
We have an issue created already

00:57:08.470 --> 00:57:10.650
called configure Gitpod.

00:57:10.650 --> 00:57:14.350
If we open that up and
as we did before we click

00:57:14.350 --> 00:57:18.050
the Gitpod button to spin
up a new environment.

00:57:19.710 --> 00:57:22.670
That takes a couple
seconds just to pull down

00:57:24.282 --> 00:57:27.230
the necessary container
image and start the IDE.

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:38.030
There we go.

00:57:38.030 --> 00:57:43.030
Now in order to configure
Gitpod, we have two options.

00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:48.410
One, we can go to Gitpod.io/new.

00:57:51.230 --> 00:57:54.470
If we go there, we can
create a new project.

00:57:54.470 --> 00:57:56.530
We can select the organization

00:57:56.530 --> 00:58:00.210
or account, and then our repository,

00:58:00.210 --> 00:58:03.130
and then follow along the instructions

00:58:04.020 --> 00:58:08.690
on the screen to configure the project.

00:58:08.690 --> 00:58:13.690
Now I prefer to add a configuration file

00:58:14.145 --> 00:58:19.000
to my source code so
that the configuration

00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:20.280
of the developer environment

00:58:20.280 --> 00:58:24.660
is also part of the revision history

00:58:24.660 --> 00:58:28.500
so we can see what changed
and when did it change

00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:31.330
and maybe also the reasons
behind some of the changes.

00:58:32.200 --> 00:58:33.870
So the way we can do that

00:58:33.870 --> 00:58:37.820
is we can create a new
file at the project route.

00:58:38.810 --> 00:58:41.490
So if we right click into
the black area around here,

00:58:41.490 --> 00:58:42.460
we say new file.

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:48.120
The file name we want
to create is .Gitpod.yml

00:58:48.120 --> 00:58:50.990
which stands for YAML, GitPod.yml

00:58:52.060 --> 00:58:55.840
And within the file here,

00:58:55.840 --> 00:58:59.890
we have certain properties
that we have to define

00:58:59.890 --> 00:59:03.040
in order for Gitpod to pick that up.

00:59:03.040 --> 00:59:05.380
What we can do is we can do autocomplete

00:59:05.380 --> 00:59:06.870
with control space,

00:59:07.940 --> 00:59:11.040
and it gives us a number
of top level properties

00:59:11.040 --> 00:59:12.060
that we can specify.

00:59:13.080 --> 00:59:16.560
Now, if we don't know what
they do, the easiest way

00:59:16.560 --> 00:59:18.770
to learn about that is to jump over

00:59:19.815 --> 00:59:21.070
to the docs on Gitpod.io/docs.

00:59:23.510 --> 00:59:28.510
And on the left side,
if we go to references,

00:59:29.470 --> 00:59:32.940
they say .Gitpod.yml reference,

00:59:32.940 --> 00:59:35.580
and you can see that all the options,

00:59:35.580 --> 00:59:39.410
including sub properties are defined

00:59:39.410 --> 00:59:41.490
and well-documented in here.

00:59:41.490 --> 00:59:45.480
So if you get stuck or if you're
not sure what to configure,

00:59:46.370 --> 00:59:50.980
this is your go-to place
for the configuration file.

00:59:53.240 --> 00:59:54.120
Now for us,

00:59:55.450 --> 00:59:57.630
I'll walk you through the key things

00:59:57.630 --> 01:00:00.150
that we need to make sure are configured.

01:00:01.080 --> 01:00:02.750
When we start a new workspace,

01:00:03.690 --> 01:00:08.250
you saw it a few minutes ago,
we have to run NPM install,

01:00:08.250 --> 01:00:10.830
and then we have to run NPM run dev,

01:00:10.830 --> 01:00:13.530
and we have to do that every time we start

01:00:13.530 --> 01:00:16.990
a new workspace just like
in your local environment,

01:00:16.990 --> 01:00:19.290
you have to start a dev server

01:00:19.290 --> 01:00:21.710
every time you work on your project.

01:00:21.710 --> 01:00:23.900
Now with Gitpod, we can automate that.

01:00:24.830 --> 01:00:29.710
So there's something
called a tasks property.

01:00:29.710 --> 01:00:33.730
And as you saw, if I use
auto complete for that,

01:00:35.360 --> 01:00:39.710
tasks, it automatically gives me a dash.

01:00:39.710 --> 01:00:42.050
And then it lets me type something here.

01:00:42.050 --> 01:00:44.230
So again, if we use auto-complete

01:00:44.230 --> 01:00:47.660
at that level, it gives me a few options.

01:00:48.560 --> 01:00:50.860
So what do we want to do for now,

01:00:50.860 --> 01:00:52.350
we'll look at other ones later,

01:00:52.350 --> 01:00:56.990
but for now we need an init
task, so we can say init.

01:00:58.000 --> 01:01:02.390
And init is something that runs every time

01:01:02.390 --> 01:01:06.560
somebody pushes code to
the GitHub repository.

01:01:07.820 --> 01:01:10.650
Imagine your colleague,
they merge a pull request

01:01:10.650 --> 01:01:14.630
into domain branch, and
as soon as that happens,

01:01:15.650 --> 01:01:19.340
something called GitPod prebuild kicks in.

01:01:19.340 --> 01:01:23.880
So GitHub notifies
Gitpod about the change,

01:01:23.880 --> 01:01:28.530
and then Gitpod kicks off
a prebuild where it looks

01:01:28.530 --> 01:01:32.250
for to GitPod YAML file and
automatically execute whatever

01:01:32.250 --> 01:01:37.040
commands we specify within the init tasks.

01:01:37.040 --> 01:01:38.620
So in our case,

01:01:38.620 --> 01:01:42.110
we want to always download
and install to dependencies

01:01:42.110 --> 01:01:45.750
for our project, as soon
as somebody changes code.

01:01:46.850 --> 01:01:50.700
So we can see NPM install
for the init task.

01:01:51.920 --> 01:01:55.140
Now there's one more thing we
want to add to that same task.

01:01:55.140 --> 01:01:58.330
So remember one dash equals to one task.

01:01:59.220 --> 01:02:03.000
The other thing we want to
add is a command right here,

01:02:04.000 --> 01:02:09.000
the command executes when
somebody starts a workspace.

01:02:09.230 --> 01:02:10.940
So at this time,

01:02:10.940 --> 01:02:14.750
NPR install already executed
on the Gitpod server

01:02:14.750 --> 01:02:18.110
at some point before
somebody starts to workspace.

01:02:19.070 --> 01:02:19.903
So what do we want to do

01:02:19.903 --> 01:02:23.440
every time somebody opens
one of these workspaces?

01:02:23.440 --> 01:02:28.030
We want to start the
development server, NPM run dev.

01:02:29.360 --> 01:02:34.360
So with that, we can
now open the workspace.

01:02:35.010 --> 01:02:36.800
Anytime we open a new workspace,

01:02:36.800 --> 01:02:39.270
the server will already be started for us,

01:02:39.270 --> 01:02:42.540
and we are ready to
start writing some code.

01:02:44.860 --> 01:02:46.310
And before we go any further,

01:02:47.190 --> 01:02:49.850
let's validate that these changes

01:02:49.850 --> 01:02:51.950
do what we expect them to do.

01:02:51.950 --> 01:02:56.360
The way to test that is to

01:02:57.370 --> 01:03:00.730
keep this developer environment,
so if we look at the URL,

01:03:00.730 --> 01:03:04.270
we are currently on an
environment called copper-thrush,

01:03:06.395 --> 01:03:07.445
we want to keep that,

01:03:08.350 --> 01:03:11.600
and we want to start a second environment

01:03:11.600 --> 01:03:14.850
where we are going to test these changes.

01:03:14.850 --> 01:03:18.280
So the way we can do that
is in source control,

01:03:19.290 --> 01:03:22.270
we can add a comment here

01:03:22.270 --> 01:03:25.160
to commit the tasks that we configured.

01:03:26.180 --> 01:03:30.590
So add init and command tasks.

01:03:32.590 --> 01:03:33.840
We can commit, then we say, yeah,

01:03:35.064 --> 01:03:36.764
please do a stage and commit that.

01:03:38.690 --> 01:03:41.710
And we create a pull
request remember, right next

01:03:41.710 --> 01:03:44.580
to the check mark, we can
say create pull request,

01:03:46.470 --> 01:03:51.470
and that's eventually we will want to call

01:03:53.095 --> 01:03:57.400
this pull request, add
Gitpod configuration.

01:03:59.960 --> 01:04:04.960
And we want to close issue number three.

01:04:06.480 --> 01:04:07.520
Remember down at the bottom,

01:04:07.520 --> 01:04:09.690
you can see in the branch name,

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:11.490
which issue number we're working on.

01:04:12.550 --> 01:04:14.780
For now, we are not done
with that pull request,

01:04:14.780 --> 01:04:16.520
we want to do more work,

01:04:16.520 --> 01:04:21.520
but we want to just test these
two tasks, these two lines.

01:04:21.600 --> 01:04:24.890
So let's make it a draft so
that everybody else on the team

01:04:24.890 --> 01:04:26.230
knows we're working on it.

01:04:27.540 --> 01:04:29.450
Yep, same message.

01:04:29.450 --> 01:04:30.720
We don't have an upstream branch,

01:04:30.720 --> 01:04:32.070
please publish that for us.

01:04:35.920 --> 01:04:38.270
All right, we don't want
to run that, all good.

01:04:39.300 --> 01:04:41.690
Ignore that error, that's not relevant.

01:04:42.640 --> 01:04:47.150
Okay, now going back here, we
do have a new pull request.

01:04:47.150 --> 01:04:49.320
Add Gitpod configuration.

01:04:49.320 --> 01:04:52.180
Here, we can see that
we added these two tasks

01:04:52.180 --> 01:04:53.540
to the GitPod YAML file.

01:04:55.260 --> 01:04:59.380
Now in order to test that new file

01:04:59.380 --> 01:05:03.787
that we created, we can go
to the conversation tab.

01:05:05.030 --> 01:05:10.030
And what we can do is start
a second Gitpod workspace.

01:05:10.960 --> 01:05:14.610
Again, remember the
URL starts with copper,

01:05:14.610 --> 01:05:19.610
and if we open a new one,
it is now ember-dragonfly.

01:05:21.710 --> 01:05:26.690
That developer environment
here is completely separate

01:05:26.690 --> 01:05:29.380
from the one we have in
the other browser tab.

01:05:29.380 --> 01:05:34.380
The differences that the
amber-dragonfly Gitpod environment

01:05:35.290 --> 01:05:39.210
uses the new configuration file

01:05:39.210 --> 01:05:42.450
that we added to the branch.

01:05:43.410 --> 01:05:47.720
So look at that, there's
some stuff from VS Code.

01:05:47.720 --> 01:05:51.290
We see that a service is
available on port 3000

01:05:51.290 --> 01:05:54.776
and in the terminal we
noticed that our application

01:05:54.776 --> 01:05:57.630
is already running on port 3000.

01:05:58.540 --> 01:06:01.630
That is pretty incredible
because we didn't actually

01:06:01.630 --> 01:06:04.380
do anything, we just automated at once,

01:06:04.380 --> 01:06:06.390
and now everybody who uses this project

01:06:07.290 --> 01:06:10.790
in a Gitpod environment gets
all of that automatically.

01:06:11.890 --> 01:06:15.250
So what is the next thing
every developer is going

01:06:16.150 --> 01:06:18.000
to do when they work on this project?

01:06:19.050 --> 01:06:21.670
Everyone who works on that and
wants to make a code change

01:06:21.670 --> 01:06:23.950
wants to validate their changes.

01:06:23.950 --> 01:06:27.060
So the very next step
literally everybody would do

01:06:28.275 --> 01:06:32.050
is open that preview URL of
our development environment.

01:06:33.800 --> 01:06:37.100
So wouldn't it be nice if we
could automate that as well?

01:06:37.100 --> 01:06:39.780
Yeah, you guessed, right,
we can definitely do that.

01:06:39.780 --> 01:06:44.320
The way we do it is that the
environment we just tested,

01:06:44.320 --> 01:06:49.120
whether the application
starts up, amber-dragonfly,

01:06:49.120 --> 01:06:51.680
we can close that we no longer need it

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:52.870
because we already tested

01:06:52.870 --> 01:06:56.460
and validated the changes
to the Gitpod YAML file.

01:06:56.460 --> 01:07:00.170
So hamburger menu, Gitpod stop workspace.

01:07:01.070 --> 01:07:03.650
We're going to throw that
away, never needed again.

01:07:04.970 --> 01:07:09.470
Now back here in the copper
environment or our developer

01:07:09.470 --> 01:07:12.650
environment where you can
clean some stuff up and back in

01:07:12.650 --> 01:07:17.650
the tasks, we can now add
something else at the top level,

01:07:18.960 --> 01:07:20.260
which is called ports.

01:07:21.460 --> 01:07:26.460
And we know that our server
starts on port 3000 right,

01:07:26.760 --> 01:07:29.720
in order to preview the web application.

01:07:29.720 --> 01:07:32.480
So we can say, Hey, Gitpod,

01:07:32.480 --> 01:07:36.810
if you see a service
listening on port 3000,

01:07:37.790 --> 01:07:40.970
then do something for
us, now we don't need

01:07:40.970 --> 01:07:43.690
the visibility private
that's the default behavior

01:07:43.690 --> 01:07:46.800
if we don't configure it,
so it will leave that out.

01:07:48.010 --> 01:07:50.650
But what we do want is control space.,

01:07:51.710 --> 01:07:53.760
and then we want to configure the onopen.

01:07:56.460 --> 01:07:59.410
Onopen, you can mouse over and
get more details about that.

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:03.395
But this is basically
when Gitpod recognizes

01:08:03.395 --> 01:08:07.060
a service listening on
the configured port,

01:08:07.060 --> 01:08:09.010
and then it will do something for us.

01:08:09.010 --> 01:08:11.020
So we have a few options here.

01:08:11.020 --> 01:08:14.300
We can ignore it, this
is helpful for, you know,

01:08:14.300 --> 01:08:18.580
a database service or something that runs

01:08:18.580 --> 01:08:21.640
to use a hot module, reloading.

01:08:21.640 --> 01:08:23.440
Ports like that we don't care about,

01:08:24.620 --> 01:08:25.920
we can simply ignore them.

01:08:26.760 --> 01:08:30.630
Notify is the default
behavior that pops up

01:08:30.630 --> 01:08:33.340
that little box at the
bottom here we saw before,

01:08:33.340 --> 01:08:35.830
where we can open in
preview or open in browser.

01:08:36.920 --> 01:08:40.890
And we have these two options
as a default here as well.

01:08:40.890 --> 01:08:41.920
So in our case,

01:08:41.920 --> 01:08:44.840
we want to open a browser
window automatically

01:08:44.840 --> 01:08:47.240
so that we don't have to click that link

01:08:47.240 --> 01:08:48.810
in the terminal, let's do that.

01:08:50.230 --> 01:08:51.100
There we go.

01:08:51.100 --> 01:08:54.660
That is now going to
open a new browser tab

01:08:54.660 --> 01:08:56.660
with the preview automatically.

01:08:58.250 --> 01:09:01.020
So let's have a look.

01:09:01.020 --> 01:09:03.610
We're going to go back
to our source control.

01:09:04.450 --> 01:09:07.650
Quickly double-check, we
added a ports, all right,

01:09:07.650 --> 01:09:12.650
so let's commit that saying
configure port 3000 behavior.

01:09:15.890 --> 01:09:17.250
Yep.

01:09:17.250 --> 01:09:18.590
Same thing.

01:09:18.590 --> 01:09:20.860
This time around, we
already have a pull request,

01:09:20.860 --> 01:09:22.860
so we don't have to create another one.

01:09:22.860 --> 01:09:25.860
All we can do is just use these three dots

01:09:27.509 --> 01:09:29.850
or click the sync changes, either way.

01:09:29.850 --> 01:09:33.940
Three dots and push or sync changes.

01:09:33.940 --> 01:09:35.450
And this will say, yup,

01:09:35.450 --> 01:09:38.080
yup, it will pull and
push all the changes.

01:09:38.080 --> 01:09:38.913
Go for it.

01:09:40.750 --> 01:09:42.380
Fabulous.

01:09:42.380 --> 01:09:46.450
We're moving back to the pull
request where we now have

01:09:46.450 --> 01:09:51.450
that configure port 3000
behavior as a new commit message.

01:09:53.970 --> 01:09:55.850
In order to test that and make sure

01:09:56.705 --> 01:10:00.570
that the developer environment
does open a new tab,

01:10:00.570 --> 01:10:03.260
same as before, we
click the Gitpod button,

01:10:04.210 --> 01:10:06.510
open a new environment,

01:10:06.510 --> 01:10:09.970
and you can see there's
a prebuild in progress.

01:10:09.970 --> 01:10:13.350
That is because my environment
is already configured.

01:10:13.350 --> 01:10:17.180
So prebuild means it is
running the init script

01:10:17.180 --> 01:10:20.400
that we configured into GitPod YAML file.

01:10:20.400 --> 01:10:23.650
That happens only once,
we were just too quick,

01:10:23.650 --> 01:10:26.300
that hasn't finished, that's
why we saw it in the URL

01:10:26.300 --> 01:10:28.560
on the UI, but there we go,

01:10:28.560 --> 01:10:32.140
now it starts coral-llama environment.

01:10:32.140 --> 01:10:34.979
Again that's the third
developer environment

01:10:34.979 --> 01:10:37.620
and it also opens a tab for our preview.

01:10:39.690 --> 01:10:42.260
That's pretty cool, it
doesn't ask us anymore

01:10:42.260 --> 01:10:44.430
whether we want to open a
preview or open a browser

01:10:44.430 --> 01:10:45.263
for this port.

01:10:46.110 --> 01:10:51.110
That's the VS Code pop
up, we can just say no.

01:10:53.100 --> 01:10:53.933
Get rid of that.

01:10:54.890 --> 01:10:55.723
Okay.

01:10:56.640 --> 01:10:59.600
That's pretty cool, now let's have a look.

01:10:59.600 --> 01:11:02.100
If we go to the preview
environment and we inspect

01:11:04.560 --> 01:11:08.240
the website, you can
see vite connecting...

01:11:10.420 --> 01:11:12.820
And if we wait a couple of seconds,

01:11:12.820 --> 01:11:15.620
it will try to reconnect again.

01:11:15.620 --> 01:11:19.570
What's happening here is
that SvelteKit under the hood

01:11:19.570 --> 01:11:22.270
uses what's called vite.

01:11:22.270 --> 01:11:26.920
And that provides a hot
module reloading functionality

01:11:26.920 --> 01:11:29.280
that we want to leverage.

01:11:30.626 --> 01:11:33.810
Now, unfortunately by default,

01:11:35.293 --> 01:11:40.293
the vite server runs on
port 3000, localhost:3000.

01:11:43.810 --> 01:11:47.440
The problem we have is
that our web address

01:11:48.877 --> 01:11:52.770
is a public URL on a Gitpod.io subdomain.

01:11:52.770 --> 01:11:54.370
So you noticed that was a reload

01:11:55.613 --> 01:11:57.813
and it didn't actually
successfully connect.

01:11:59.100 --> 01:12:00.200
That's not a big deal.

01:12:01.190 --> 01:12:04.280
We can configure vite in
order to do the right thing,

01:12:04.280 --> 01:12:08.070
it keeps doing that reloading
here until we configure it.

01:12:09.050 --> 01:12:14.050
So we are running on an HTTPS URL

01:12:14.950 --> 01:12:18.680
that is this super long URL here

01:12:18.680 --> 01:12:23.490
and no port, which means
HTTPS default port is 443.

01:12:23.490 --> 01:12:25.110
So what we need to do is we need

01:12:27.140 --> 01:12:31.167
to somehow configure
SvelteKit to use this URL,

01:12:32.850 --> 01:12:36.190
to connect to the hot
module reloading server,

01:12:36.190 --> 01:12:40.220
instead of using
localhost 3000 by default.

01:12:40.220 --> 01:12:42.270
Let's have a look and how we can do that.

01:12:45.450 --> 01:12:47.340
First we are going to stop the server

01:12:48.540 --> 01:12:50.726
and what are we going to do next

01:12:50.726 --> 01:12:55.110
is we're going to close
or actually keep the same

01:12:55.110 --> 01:12:58.140
environment here, remember
the old one we had,

01:12:58.140 --> 01:13:00.770
where we configured the tasks

01:13:00.770 --> 01:13:02.740
and then we pushed it to GitHub?

01:13:03.870 --> 01:13:08.340
That environment is something
we could keep working on,

01:13:09.280 --> 01:13:13.430
but it doesn't really make much
sense because it's outdated

01:13:13.430 --> 01:13:16.740
by now, we started up
the new environment based

01:13:16.740 --> 01:13:18.750
on the new configuration.

01:13:18.750 --> 01:13:20.930
So we can basically just stop this,

01:13:21.910 --> 01:13:26.260
stop this environment now
and no longer worry about it.

01:13:27.820 --> 01:13:30.810
We'll shut that down.
Close the browser tab.

01:13:32.750 --> 01:13:37.750
Now we are working on that
pull request that we opened up.

01:13:39.400 --> 01:13:42.090
Okay, so we stopped the server down here.

01:13:42.090 --> 01:13:45.960
And the next thing is in
the Svelte config file,

01:13:45.960 --> 01:13:50.470
we can come in here and down in the,

01:13:52.946 --> 01:13:57.063
in the kit configuration,
we can add a new value.

01:13:58.540 --> 01:14:01.690
So what do we want to do here is vite

01:14:03.860 --> 01:14:05.710
and then it's called server, you can see,

01:14:05.710 --> 01:14:07.710
we get nice autocomplete for everything.

01:14:08.550 --> 01:14:11.560
And then the property is HMR
for hot module reloading.

01:14:13.410 --> 01:14:15.310
There's two properties we want to set.

01:14:16.390 --> 01:14:18.960
The first is client port, as I mentioned,

01:14:18.960 --> 01:14:23.390
the default is 3000 and the
other one we want to set

01:14:24.758 --> 01:14:25.790
is the host.

01:14:26.740 --> 01:14:29.280
And again, by default, this is localhost.

01:14:32.820 --> 01:14:37.557
The question is, how do we
change this port to 443,

01:14:39.810 --> 01:14:43.670
and this host to whatever
URL we have here?

01:14:43.670 --> 01:14:46.400
That URL changes for each environment,

01:14:46.400 --> 01:14:50.470
so we need to find a
way to dynamically load

01:14:50.470 --> 01:14:55.470
the URL and configure
the Svelte environment.

01:14:56.990 --> 01:15:01.300
So luckily the Gitpod environment provides

01:15:01.300 --> 01:15:05.480
a command line interface that's called GP.

01:15:06.360 --> 01:15:09.090
So if we open the terminal
a bit bigger here,

01:15:09.090 --> 01:15:11.110
I can show you how this looks.

01:15:11.110 --> 01:15:12.000
Let's clear this.

01:15:12.860 --> 01:15:17.860
If we type gp url, that gives us the URL

01:15:19.980 --> 01:15:23.410
of our environment, matches
the URL up here, right?

01:15:24.580 --> 01:15:27.820
However, our URL has the port in front.

01:15:28.960 --> 01:15:33.110
So gp url accepts an argument,

01:15:33.110 --> 01:15:35.950
if we pass the port number at the end

01:15:35.950 --> 01:15:37.260
and we hit enter again,

01:15:38.240 --> 01:15:43.240
it gives us the preview URL of
that port that we specified.

01:15:44.040 --> 01:15:48.660
And this is the URL that we
want to specify for the host,

01:15:49.930 --> 01:15:51.490
and then port 443.

01:15:53.802 --> 01:15:56.260
We could somehow try to hard-code that,

01:15:56.260 --> 01:15:59.740
but not everybody uses Gitpod yet.

01:15:59.740 --> 01:16:03.130
So we have to also make sure
localhosts 3000 is configured

01:16:03.130 --> 01:16:06.060
for people who work on
their local computer still.

01:16:07.080 --> 01:16:09.130
Let's have a look, how we can do that.

01:16:09.130 --> 01:16:14.130
The easiest way is to use
an environment variable

01:16:14.360 --> 01:16:19.130
where we specify whether
we run on Gitpod or not.

01:16:19.130 --> 01:16:23.360
So the place to do that is
in the Gitpod YAML file,

01:16:25.870 --> 01:16:29.210
what we can do here is in the command.

01:16:29.210 --> 01:16:34.210
So when the new development
environment starts up,

01:16:35.010 --> 01:16:40.010
we want to run that gp url 3000 command

01:16:42.330 --> 01:16:46.050
and save the results, that URL
in an environment variable.

01:16:47.000 --> 01:16:50.000
That means if somebody
starts the environment

01:16:50.000 --> 01:16:52.240
in their local computer,

01:16:52.240 --> 01:16:55.070
none of the code in the
Gitpod YAML executes

01:16:55.070 --> 01:16:57.520
because they use a local environment,

01:16:57.520 --> 01:17:00.380
they don't get the benefits
of this automation,

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:03.070
which then means the environment
variable that we're about

01:17:03.070 --> 01:17:07.920
to set does not exist on the
localhost, let's have a look

01:17:07.920 --> 01:17:10.310
how this looks like.

01:17:10.310 --> 01:17:14.410
So here we want to do export,
let's call it HMR_HOST

01:17:15.860 --> 01:17:20.420
and that equals, and then
two back ticks, gp url 3000.

01:17:22.460 --> 01:17:26.110
And then start the dev server.

01:17:27.100 --> 01:17:30.560
Now, HMR host after this line

01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:35.280
looks equals to this entire URL, HTTPS,

01:17:35.280 --> 01:17:36.130
blah, blah, blah.

01:17:37.270 --> 01:17:38.103
So let's have a look.

01:17:38.103 --> 01:17:43.030
We can take that HMR host
and in the Svelte config,

01:17:45.890 --> 01:17:49.790
since this is just a regular
JavaScript that executes,

01:17:49.790 --> 01:17:54.790
we can use the process.env for
all the environment variables

01:17:57.716 --> 01:18:02.716
and have a look at
whether HMR host exists.

01:18:04.620 --> 01:18:06.180
So we can just do a shorthand,

01:18:07.210 --> 01:18:10.180
which means if that URL exists

01:18:13.440 --> 01:18:17.610
then process env host,
otherwise localhost.

01:18:19.160 --> 01:18:24.160
So that means we can now run
this script either locally

01:18:25.030 --> 01:18:27.260
without Gitpod or on Gitpod.

01:18:27.260 --> 01:18:29.300
And the host is always set correctly.

01:18:30.170 --> 01:18:34.510
One small tweak we need
to do is that the HMR host

01:18:35.700 --> 01:18:39.840
is including the HTTPS
or the protocol as well.

01:18:40.690 --> 01:18:41.950
vite doesn't like that.

01:18:41.950 --> 01:18:45.640
It just wants to host
because it uses web sockets.

01:18:45.640 --> 01:18:50.640
So it will then change HTTPS
to web socket protocol.

01:18:51.000 --> 01:18:52.600
So we can use JavaScript here

01:18:53.460 --> 01:18:58.460
to basically just substring the HTTPS://

01:19:01.030 --> 01:19:06.030
So we'll do sub string
and HTTPS:// .length.

01:19:09.890 --> 01:19:14.630
This will get rid of that of that part.

01:19:16.310 --> 01:19:19.510
What I mean is .length in here,

01:19:19.510 --> 01:19:22.180
so that it counts the character here,

01:19:22.180 --> 01:19:26.250
and then start from the
beginning of that URL gets starts

01:19:26.250 --> 01:19:29.560
at this point here, and it
takes 3000 and the rest.

01:19:29.560 --> 01:19:33.670
The other thing we want to
do is if we have the HMR host

01:19:33.670 --> 01:19:38.670
variable set right here,
then the port is 443.

01:19:40.640 --> 01:19:44.610
Otherwise the port is 3000.

