01:46:03.780 --> 01:46:07.480
As you can imagine, adding three of them

01:46:07.480 --> 01:46:11.370
renders three todo-item lines.

01:46:14.160 --> 01:46:16.160
So I'm going to show you one more thing.

01:46:17.250 --> 01:46:21.742
If we were to move things around,

01:46:21.742 --> 01:46:25.890
such as let's say, we
moved out todo application

01:46:25.890 --> 01:46:30.650
into like sub folders
or change things around.

01:46:30.650 --> 01:46:35.650
What we would have to do is go
and update the relative path

01:46:36.460 --> 01:46:39.920
that is used in our input statement here.

01:46:39.920 --> 01:46:41.740
So in SvelteKit,

01:46:41.740 --> 01:46:44.760
there is a convention where

01:46:44.760 --> 01:46:47.840
if we want to access anything
in the lib directory,

01:46:48.990 --> 01:46:51.720
we can just remove the relative path

01:46:51.720 --> 01:46:54.630
and use the dollar sign.

01:46:54.630 --> 01:46:59.250
So a $lib, that automatically points

01:47:00.420 --> 01:47:02.900
to the source lib, you can see
that up here in the tool tip.

01:47:02.900 --> 01:47:07.900
It points to the source
lib todo-item as well.

01:47:08.130 --> 01:47:11.180
So it automatically replaces that.

01:47:11.180 --> 01:47:15.780
Now we can move that input
statement anywhere in our code,

01:47:15.780 --> 01:47:20.530
and it will automatically
resolve to the correct endpoint

01:47:20.530 --> 01:47:23.030
or directory here in the file system.

01:47:23.900 --> 01:47:27.550
So that's a neat little trick to help us

01:47:28.780 --> 01:47:29.780
load that todo-item.

01:47:32.100 --> 01:47:33.190
There's one more thing we want to do

01:47:33.190 --> 01:47:35.940
while we're working on the HTML structure.

01:47:35.940 --> 01:47:40.480
It's setting a title for the application.

01:47:40.480 --> 01:47:44.770
At the moment, if we inspect
that and we go to the head,

01:47:46.400 --> 01:47:49.050
you can see that there
there's some meta tags.

01:47:49.050 --> 01:47:52.700
They come from our source/app.html.

01:47:53.640 --> 01:47:58.640
There's some scripts, some
styles, but there's no title.

01:47:59.220 --> 01:48:04.220
So remember if we go to src/app.html

01:48:06.400 --> 01:48:09.070
there's the meta tags I just showed you.

01:48:09.070 --> 01:48:11.050
There's no title, we could add one here,

01:48:11.050 --> 01:48:12.540
but then it would be the same title

01:48:12.540 --> 01:48:15.230
for the entire application.

01:48:15.230 --> 01:48:16.990
So in order to populate

01:48:16.990 --> 01:48:19.290
that dynamically created Svelte head,

01:48:20.550 --> 01:48:25.550
what we can do in our index
file somewhere up here is

01:48:27.230 --> 01:48:30.350
use a special component
called Svelte colon,

01:48:30.350 --> 01:48:32.270
and you can see there's a few of them.

01:48:32.270 --> 01:48:36.310
The one we care about right
now is head, we use that.

01:48:37.500 --> 01:48:41.950
We can now put any HTML
elements that are valid

01:48:41.950 --> 01:48:45.520
within the head inside of here and Svelte

01:48:45.520 --> 01:48:47.620
will dynamically populate that

01:48:47.620 --> 01:48:50.160
when the different pages load.

01:48:50.160 --> 01:48:52.660
So the element we want to add is title,

01:48:53.620 --> 01:48:56.000
and we want to call it todos.

01:48:56.000 --> 01:48:58.760
Now you notice that it already reloaded,

01:48:58.760 --> 01:49:00.640
hot module reloading kicked in,

01:49:00.640 --> 01:49:02.390
and the title now says todos.

01:49:03.260 --> 01:49:06.920
And if we update that and we save,

01:49:06.920 --> 01:49:09.330
then it immediately updates as well.

01:49:10.730 --> 01:49:11.900
So that's nice,

01:49:11.900 --> 01:49:16.320
but let's make an agreement that the title

01:49:16.320 --> 01:49:21.320
should always reflect whatever
the H1 element is here.

01:49:21.440 --> 01:49:24.670
Okay, so that the H1 on the page

01:49:24.670 --> 01:49:26.670
maps to the title in the browser window,

01:49:28.140 --> 01:49:31.120
that's happening right now
because we wrote todos twice.

01:49:31.120 --> 01:49:32.340
Fair enough, but if we were

01:49:32.340 --> 01:49:35.500
to change that todos title here,

01:49:35.500 --> 01:49:38.520
we would have to remember
to also change it here.

01:49:38.520 --> 01:49:41.310
So I'll show you how you
can use the variables

01:49:41.310 --> 01:49:43.780
in Svelte and reference
them within the HTML.

01:49:44.940 --> 01:49:47.060
Let's create a variable called title.

01:49:47.940 --> 01:49:52.570
And the name for that
is order value is todo

01:49:53.840 --> 01:49:56.750
by the way that name, variable
name could be anything.

01:49:56.750 --> 01:49:59.300
It could be page, title
whatever you want it to be.

01:50:01.415 --> 01:50:04.320
In order to use it in your HTML,

01:50:04.320 --> 01:50:08.450
we can select these two
with command D or control D

01:50:09.640 --> 01:50:14.390
to have multiple cursors, so
we delete the hard-coded texts.

01:50:14.390 --> 01:50:18.680
We use opening curly
brackets, variable name,

01:50:18.680 --> 01:50:20.730
closing curly brackets.

01:50:20.730 --> 01:50:24.440
And now you can see
the todo here and here,

01:50:24.440 --> 01:50:25.340
is still the same.

01:50:26.310 --> 01:50:31.310
If we were to make a change,
then the browser updates,

01:50:31.570 --> 01:50:33.680
as well as the todo-item here.

01:50:34.910 --> 01:50:38.650
So this is how you can use variables

01:50:38.650 --> 01:50:42.950
within your HTML in a Svelte component.

01:50:44.000 --> 01:50:47.020
So we'll keep it like that,
that's probably a good start.

01:50:47.020 --> 01:50:49.270
And it's a good reference as well

01:50:49.270 --> 01:50:51.550
if you want to update that later.

01:50:53.430 --> 01:50:55.810
Excellent, I think this is a good spot

