01:54:02.800 --> 01:54:03.950
If you remember,

01:54:03.950 --> 01:54:07.210
this is the end result of
what we want to achieve

01:54:08.990 --> 01:54:11.030
and let's get started with that.

01:54:11.030 --> 01:54:13.990
So we have an issue, apply CSS styles,

01:54:13.990 --> 01:54:17.170
where we can start a developer environment

01:54:17.170 --> 01:54:20.380
and continue with our work.

01:54:22.340 --> 01:54:24.040
What are we going to learn here is

01:54:25.850 --> 01:54:29.360
how to apply styles in Svelte components.

01:54:29.360 --> 01:54:32.190
There's a few options, different ways.

01:54:32.190 --> 01:54:33.810
We'll talk about that.

01:54:33.810 --> 01:54:36.470
And basically the end result is we want

01:54:36.470 --> 01:54:39.150
to turn what do we currently have here,

01:54:39.150 --> 01:54:42.580
the unstyled todo application,

01:54:42.580 --> 01:54:46.100
we want to turn that into what we see

01:54:47.070 --> 01:54:49.230
right here in the mock-up.

01:54:50.790 --> 01:54:54.130
Okay, switching over to
our developer environment.

01:54:55.320 --> 01:54:58.080
We don't need any of these
things at the moment, the server

01:54:58.080 --> 01:55:01.490
is already running, so we'll
just keep that up and running.

01:55:02.590 --> 01:55:05.070
Maybe the first thing we can do is inspect

01:55:05.070 --> 01:55:10.070
the web application so that
we can then look at CSS,

01:55:11.550 --> 01:55:16.550
different styles and keep
an eye on what's happening.

01:55:17.090 --> 01:55:19.370
So with that,

01:55:19.370 --> 01:55:24.000
let's get started and apply
CSS to this application.

01:55:27.120 --> 01:55:31.823
The first thing I want to
show you is how we can use

01:55:33.260 --> 01:55:37.330
regular CSS files to style the application

01:55:38.563 --> 01:55:41.240
and use the styles that we add

01:55:41.240 --> 01:55:44.460
across the entire application
so that we can put

01:55:44.460 --> 01:55:49.080
certain default behavior in
place that applies to the entire

01:55:49.080 --> 01:55:53.457
application where like we
define style attributes

01:55:55.400 --> 01:55:58.450
such as the font family or primary colors

01:55:58.450 --> 01:55:59.930
and things like that.

01:55:59.930 --> 01:56:04.930
So we want to do this by
creating a CSS file under source.

01:56:07.260 --> 01:56:10.400
So let's say source and then new file,

01:56:10.400 --> 01:56:12.940
and we'll call this app.CSS.

01:56:15.050 --> 01:56:17.160
Okay, this is a regular CSS,

01:56:17.160 --> 01:56:20.880
there's nothing fancy about this.

01:56:21.940 --> 01:56:26.610
The first thing we can do is
turn that white background

01:56:26.610 --> 01:56:31.610
that we currently have into
more of this gradient approach

01:56:32.080 --> 01:56:35.360
that you see in the mock-up.

01:56:36.940 --> 01:56:38.430
The way to do that is

01:56:39.390 --> 01:56:43.210
we target the body element
of the web application.

01:56:44.080 --> 01:56:49.080
And for now we can just say
the background color equals to

01:56:53.200 --> 01:56:57.823
a hash value is so B9C6D2,
that could be, you know,

01:56:59.540 --> 01:57:03.410
whatever you need for
your environment later.

01:57:04.700 --> 01:57:06.620
So with that,

01:57:06.620 --> 01:57:09.380
it's still white because we
haven't actually loaded that CSS

01:57:09.380 --> 01:57:14.380
file, the app.css is an
arbitrary name that I chose.

01:57:14.500 --> 01:57:17.460
You could name this
file anything you like.

01:57:18.520 --> 01:57:19.700
So before we go any further,

01:57:19.700 --> 01:57:22.410
let's just make sure the changes we apply

01:57:22.410 --> 01:57:25.780
are actually visible in
the developer environment.

01:57:25.780 --> 01:57:29.930
So in our case, we have a
source roots index file.

01:57:31.190 --> 01:57:34.520
And in this file,

01:57:34.520 --> 01:57:39.520
what we can do is import the
CSS file that we just created

01:57:39.600 --> 01:57:43.640
a little while ago, so
if we go to the script,

01:57:43.640 --> 01:57:46.910
this is where we import the todo component

01:57:46.910 --> 01:57:51.710
as you remember, we can
also import the CSS file,

01:57:53.320 --> 01:57:56.600
which lives two directories up.

01:57:56.600 --> 01:58:00.790
And there is the, oops, my apologies,

01:58:00.790 --> 01:58:04.450
it's one directory up and app.CSS.

01:58:05.420 --> 01:58:09.550
So with that, if we switch
back to the todo application,

01:58:09.550 --> 01:58:12.890
we now have the grayish background.

01:58:14.040 --> 01:58:17.600
So that's one way to load the CSS file,

01:58:18.590 --> 01:58:21.330
now, one thing I want to show you is

01:58:22.756 --> 01:58:23.860
if we were to add a second route,

01:58:23.860 --> 01:58:25.720
so under source routes, new file,

01:58:26.610 --> 01:58:30.200
we call this about us just as a test

01:58:33.050 --> 01:58:35.320
and within the about us file, we create

01:58:36.350 --> 01:58:38.900
the header for now that we
see, we changed the URL.

01:58:40.230 --> 01:58:42.440
If I were to switch to about us,

01:58:45.000 --> 01:58:49.100
it did not apply that change
to the CSS background here.

01:58:49.100 --> 01:58:52.190
And that's because as you
can guess in the index file,

01:58:52.190 --> 01:58:56.620
we import a file, we
don't do that in about us.

01:58:56.620 --> 01:58:59.210
I could create a script
tag and import the CSS file

01:58:59.210 --> 01:59:02.800
as well, but that would
not be very scalable.

01:59:02.800 --> 01:59:07.200
So instead there is a
special file we can create

01:59:07.200 --> 01:59:10.540
at any level within the roots tree.

01:59:10.540 --> 01:59:11.940
So if there were subdirectories,

01:59:11.940 --> 01:59:14.979
we could create more of these files

01:59:14.979 --> 01:59:17.570
and the file is called __layout

01:59:18.430 --> 01:59:23.240
So we within SRC/roots, __layout

01:59:23.240 --> 01:59:28.240
that Svelte this file
contains code that applies

01:59:30.010 --> 01:59:33.970
to all the routes within the
directory where it's defined

01:59:33.970 --> 01:59:37.530
as well as all sub-directories below that.

01:59:38.860 --> 01:59:42.170
So what we can do here is we can

01:59:44.715 --> 01:59:47.950
import that CSS file at the layout level,

01:59:47.950 --> 01:59:51.570
which then applies to about
us and index and anything else

01:59:51.570 --> 01:59:54.160
within that directory
or its sub-directories.

01:59:55.560 --> 01:59:57.380
So going back here,

01:59:57.380 --> 02:00:00.900
let's remove the CSS
import from the index file.

02:00:02.420 --> 02:00:03.600
And in the layout,

02:00:05.070 --> 02:00:06.960
we are going to create a script tag

02:00:09.487 --> 02:00:12.000
and import that CSS from here.

02:00:13.120 --> 02:00:15.150
If we go back to the user interface,

02:00:16.110 --> 02:00:20.520
we noticed that the about us
page now shows the background,

02:00:20.520 --> 02:00:24.360
but it doesn't show the title anymore.

02:00:24.360 --> 02:00:28.890
And the same is true on the
index page, completely empty,

02:00:28.890 --> 02:00:30.750
but the background is there.

02:00:30.750 --> 02:00:35.750
So we need to render the content

02:00:36.700 --> 02:00:38.870
of whichever page we are on,

02:00:38.870 --> 02:00:40.250
whether we were on the route page

02:00:40.250 --> 02:00:42.210
or on the about us page.

02:00:42.210 --> 02:00:46.980
We need to tell Svelte to
render the content dynamically

02:00:46.980 --> 02:00:48.830
depending on the URL.

02:00:48.830 --> 02:00:51.990
And the way we can do that
in the layout component

02:00:51.990 --> 02:00:54.860
is by using what is called a slot.

02:00:54.860 --> 02:00:57.122
So S-L-O-T as a component

02:00:57.122 --> 02:01:01.050
and now Svelte dynamically
replaces line number five

02:01:01.050 --> 02:01:05.690
with the content of either the index file

02:01:05.690 --> 02:01:09.420
or the about us page,
depending on the URL we are on.

02:01:09.420 --> 02:01:14.420
If we switched back, we
have our style todos back

02:01:15.840 --> 02:01:18.810
and on the about us
page, we have the title.

02:01:20.150 --> 02:01:24.570
All right, that is a
good place to keep that.

02:01:24.570 --> 02:01:29.570
If we wanted to add additional
HTML into the layout file

02:01:31.359 --> 02:01:34.650
that applies to the other
files in the routes,

02:01:34.650 --> 02:01:38.880
we could do things like
wrap the slot in the main

02:01:40.090 --> 02:01:44.330
HTML element, and maybe
below we could add a footer

02:01:44.330 --> 02:01:48.980
where we say copyright,
something like that.

02:01:48.980 --> 02:01:53.750
And then in the UI, the, if we were to,

02:01:55.010 --> 02:02:00.010
if we were to save that
and then go back to the UI,

02:02:00.030 --> 02:02:03.500
we would now have the
copyright here, the footer,

02:02:03.500 --> 02:02:07.520
as well as on the index
page copyright as well.

02:02:08.700 --> 02:02:12.420
Okay, that gives you a good
idea of how you can extend the

02:02:12.420 --> 02:02:17.207
application and share some
code across multiple routes.

02:02:18.060 --> 02:02:22.350
As always the Svelte
documentation at Svelte.dev c

02:02:22.350 --> 02:02:26.050
ontains a lot more information
about the feature of slots

02:02:26.050 --> 02:02:29.200
and how you can use them
in your own components

02:02:29.200 --> 02:02:32.190
to nest certain functionality,

02:02:32.190 --> 02:02:36.720
as well as how you pass prompts
from the parent component

02:02:36.720 --> 02:02:38.580
to the slot and vice versa.

02:02:39.420 --> 02:02:41.990
For the time being we
don't really have a footer,

02:02:41.990 --> 02:02:43.240
so let's get rid of that.

02:02:44.080 --> 02:02:46.200
We also don't have an about us page,

02:02:46.200 --> 02:02:47.940
so we will drop that as well,

02:02:49.360 --> 02:02:52.880
but we will keep the main and
the slots so that we can use

02:02:52.880 --> 02:02:56.790
our CSS file back on the index page.

02:02:58.120 --> 02:03:00.747
Good, so we know we no longer need that

02:03:00.747 --> 02:03:04.560
and we don't need this index
file at the moment either.

02:03:05.960 --> 02:03:09.510
Good, so this is a good place to be,

02:03:09.510 --> 02:03:12.960
to understand how we can load the CSS.

02:03:12.960 --> 02:03:17.603
Now, what do we want to do
is apply a few more things

02:03:18.700 --> 02:03:23.640
to the website in terms of
styling, we're not quite done.

02:03:23.640 --> 02:03:27.820
We are actually far from
done, so back in the CSS file,

02:03:29.250 --> 02:03:30.280
what we can do is first

02:03:30.280 --> 02:03:35.280
of all, this color is our primary color

02:03:35.840 --> 02:03:38.790
that we want to use
across the application.

02:03:38.790 --> 02:03:43.790
So in order to not have
to repeat that hex value,

02:03:43.850 --> 02:03:46.300
we can use is pseudo element route.

02:03:47.910 --> 02:03:49.776
And then we can specify

02:03:49.776 --> 02:03:53.433
a CSS property here called --primary-color

02:03:58.585 --> 02:04:00.790
and use that hex value
that we have down here

02:04:02.220 --> 02:04:06.950
to define the primary color value.

02:04:06.950 --> 02:04:11.180
And now anywhere in our CSS, we can,

02:04:11.180 --> 02:04:12.840
instead of copying that hex

02:04:12.840 --> 02:04:16.280
we can just use the
primary color variable.

02:04:16.280 --> 02:04:20.760
So we'll do var and we use primary color,

02:04:20.760 --> 02:04:22.990
autocomplete makes that super easy.

02:04:24.730 --> 02:04:29.730
So in order to create the
gradient that you see here,

02:04:30.570 --> 02:04:35.220
we need at least one more
color so that we can specify

02:04:35.220 --> 02:04:38.320
the gradient from this point to this point

02:04:38.320 --> 02:04:39.530
in terms of the colors.

02:04:40.770 --> 02:04:42.330
But we are going to add two more,

02:04:42.330 --> 02:04:47.330
just so that we have three
colors, so secondary color,

02:04:48.000 --> 02:04:51.530
and the value for that is D0DDE9.

02:04:54.350 --> 02:04:57.230
Again, these are just values
I took from the example,

02:04:57.230 --> 02:05:00.310
but you can use your own brand colors.

02:05:00.310 --> 02:05:04.180
And lastly, the third one, tertiary color,

02:05:05.620 --> 02:05:09.157
and for that, we used
the hash EDF0F8, nice.

02:05:14.220 --> 02:05:17.640
So this is not really a CSS tutorial,

02:05:17.640 --> 02:05:21.390
but just make sure the
application looks all right,

02:05:21.390 --> 02:05:26.390
we'll apply a background and
we can use the linear gradient

02:05:28.120 --> 02:05:32.123
for that, and just add a few values here.

02:05:34.100 --> 02:05:38.060
So we wanna rotate by 180 degrees

02:05:38.060 --> 02:05:41.560
and then use the three
colors that we defined

02:05:41.560 --> 02:05:45.857
to specify how will we are styling this.

02:05:47.470 --> 02:05:52.470
So let's have a look here, and secondary.

02:05:56.390 --> 02:06:01.220
And the last one, there we go.

02:06:01.220 --> 02:06:02.510
Now, if we go back,

02:06:02.510 --> 02:06:06.760
we have the gradient that you can see.

02:06:07.870 --> 02:06:09.990
The problem is that it happens

02:06:09.990 --> 02:06:12.530
kind of three times, two and a half times.

02:06:13.500 --> 02:06:18.260
And the reason for that is that our body

02:06:19.642 --> 02:06:23.170
is not really spanning the
entire height of the window.

02:06:23.170 --> 02:06:28.170
So we can fix that fairly
easily by updating our body

02:06:28.720 --> 02:06:33.720
definition and add a min height
of 100 view point height.

02:06:37.610 --> 02:06:40.580
And now if we look at
that we get that gradient

02:06:40.580 --> 02:06:44.190
across the three colors, nicely applied.

02:06:44.190 --> 02:06:45.940
The other thing I want to do is see

02:06:45.940 --> 02:06:47.930
that marching on the left here?

02:06:47.930 --> 02:06:49.700
I want to take full control over that

02:06:49.700 --> 02:06:52.470
and not let the browser interfere.

02:06:52.470 --> 02:06:56.840
So one workaround for that is
to just basically set a margin

02:06:56.840 --> 02:07:01.840
of zero at this level so that we can use

02:07:03.200 --> 02:07:05.990
our own margins eventually to apply here.

02:07:05.990 --> 02:07:08.710
Now you see there's aligned
all the way to the left.

02:07:10.160 --> 02:07:13.810
Good, so this app.css file
is a good place to define

02:07:15.683 --> 02:07:20.683
all sorts of global CSS properties.

02:07:21.330 --> 02:07:24.454
One more thing we could do is,

02:07:24.454 --> 02:07:26.360
all of that doesn't make
too much of a difference,

02:07:26.360 --> 02:07:28.910
but just want to show you
in case you have your own

02:07:29.750 --> 02:07:33.180
custom fonts, so we could
specify the font family here,

02:07:34.550 --> 02:07:38.240
and I don't know, pick
one or use your own.

02:07:38.240 --> 02:07:41.390
If you have your own font that applies

02:07:41.390 --> 02:07:42.940
as well to everything.

02:07:45.170 --> 02:07:50.170
Good, one other thing we
want to do if we look at

02:07:50.950 --> 02:07:55.950
this page versus the mock-up,
the todo text is centered,

02:07:58.550 --> 02:08:03.550
and we can just agree now
that this is a design decision

02:08:03.850 --> 02:08:08.510
that we want to apply
to all the H1 elements

02:08:08.510 --> 02:08:10.660
across the entire project,

02:08:10.660 --> 02:08:13.890
once we add more pages,
we want the same behavior.

02:08:13.890 --> 02:08:18.540
So again the app CSS is a
fantastic place to configure that.

02:08:20.402 --> 02:08:25.402
We can say H1 and text align
and then make this center

02:08:27.280 --> 02:08:32.280
as well as just specify a
font size of, let's say 2rem.

02:08:34.820 --> 02:08:37.400
This then applies a cross the board

02:08:39.131 --> 02:08:42.540
and makes all our pages
look the same, exactly same.

02:08:45.640 --> 02:08:47.640
And just because we are here,

02:08:48.810 --> 02:08:53.810
if you wanted to apply a
media query or really any CSS,

02:08:54.560 --> 02:08:56.980
because that's just a regular CSS file,

02:08:56.980 --> 02:09:01.460
there's no special use case or anything.

02:09:01.460 --> 02:09:03.250
We can use media queries as well,

02:09:04.883 --> 02:09:07.900
if we were inclined to do so.

02:09:07.900 --> 02:09:11.280
For example, we can change,
if we had a min width of,

02:09:11.280 --> 02:09:12.930
let's say 720 pixels,

02:09:14.250 --> 02:09:19.250
we can apply a different
font size, for example,

02:09:19.450 --> 02:09:24.450
for the H1, as you know,
something for you to work with.

02:09:25.940 --> 02:09:29.050
So anything you can do
in CSS, you can do here.

02:09:29.050 --> 02:09:32.870
It applies to the todo-item depending

02:09:32.870 --> 02:09:35.520
on the media query that applies.

02:09:39.450 --> 02:09:41.830
Okay with that, we learned
how we can apply CSS

02:09:41.830 --> 02:09:42.680
across the board.

02:09:45.925 --> 02:09:49.460
Now for the styling of
the actual todo app here,

02:09:49.460 --> 02:09:50.900
so the different rows,

02:09:51.760 --> 02:09:55.540
the opacity here, the
margins, all these things.

02:09:57.960 --> 02:10:00.888
That is CSS that we don't want to apply

02:10:00.888 --> 02:10:02.810
to all the pages, right?

02:10:02.810 --> 02:10:07.000
The todo application may live on one URL

02:10:08.070 --> 02:10:10.140
maybe slash todo or something like that,

02:10:10.140 --> 02:10:15.140
and we only want this CSS to
impact that one component,

02:10:17.610 --> 02:10:21.400
which also means that
the browser doesn't have

02:10:21.400 --> 02:10:26.400
to download that CSS for every
page the user navigates to.

02:10:26.960 --> 02:10:29.290
We only want the browser to download

02:10:29.290 --> 02:10:32.830
that check mark, for example,
when a user actually looks

02:10:32.830 --> 02:10:36.150
at the todo application, so with Svelte,

02:10:36.150 --> 02:10:40.710
the way we can do that is
straight in a Svelte component,

02:10:40.710 --> 02:10:45.710
we can write CSS and make sure
that this applies only there.

02:10:47.300 --> 02:10:52.300
So let's have a look at the
index file under source route.

02:10:53.100 --> 02:10:55.350
So we don't need the
global CSS file anymore,

02:10:56.460 --> 02:11:01.460
but under source, roots,
index.Svelte, we can,

02:11:04.340 --> 02:11:08.110
besides the script and the
HTML, we can add one more thing.

02:11:08.110 --> 02:11:12.550
And that is a style tag.

02:11:12.550 --> 02:11:16.670
So a regular HTML style element,

02:11:18.260 --> 02:11:22.623
and within this, we can use CSS to target

02:11:23.580 --> 02:11:26.700
our element within that component.

02:11:26.700 --> 02:11:31.700
For example, if I were to
target a form like this

02:11:35.290 --> 02:11:39.320
in regular CSS, if it's
in the app.CSS file,

02:11:40.200 --> 02:11:43.900
anything I put in here into
that definition would apply

02:11:43.900 --> 02:11:47.000
across the board, across
the entire application.

02:11:47.000 --> 02:11:50.900
Because I'm specifying this style element

02:11:50.900 --> 02:11:55.260
within my component or my route
here, anything I put here,

02:11:55.260 --> 02:11:58.410
even though I use form
like this as an element,

02:11:59.420 --> 02:12:03.850
it only applies to that
form within the component.

02:12:03.850 --> 02:12:07.350
So any CSS is locally
scoped to the component,

02:12:07.350 --> 02:12:11.090
which makes it very easy to
reason about what happens

02:12:11.090 --> 02:12:13.400
if I change anything in
this form definition,

02:12:14.270 --> 02:12:17.230
it will not impact anything
outside the component.

02:12:17.230 --> 02:12:19.820
Super easy to make changes down the road

02:12:19.820 --> 02:12:21.690
without worrying that, you know,

02:12:21.690 --> 02:12:25.530
you accidentally may have
broken the form layout

02:12:25.530 --> 02:12:28.490
on a different page or a
different part of the application.

02:12:30.190 --> 02:12:32.620
Now we are not gonna
target the form like this,

02:12:32.620 --> 02:12:35.350
but I wanted to show you how this works.

02:12:35.350 --> 02:12:39.740
What we are going to do
though is basically wrapped

02:12:39.740 --> 02:12:42.120
the entire application in a div

02:12:43.020 --> 02:12:47.160
just so that we can target
it and apply things like

02:12:47.160 --> 02:12:50.630
centering of the element
and things like that.

02:12:50.630 --> 02:12:55.627
So let's first create that div here

02:12:57.190 --> 02:13:02.190
and then move our entire
application inside of that div

02:13:04.390 --> 02:13:08.760
so same code as before, but
wrapped in a div element.

02:13:08.760 --> 02:13:13.420
Now let's give that a
class, so class todos.

02:13:14.960 --> 02:13:18.610
And now within the style
element that we defined here,

02:13:18.610 --> 02:13:23.610
we can target that
todos div and apply CSS.

02:13:27.520 --> 02:13:30.330
The few things we want
to do here is we want

02:13:30.330 --> 02:13:34.200
to specify a with of 100%.

02:13:36.150 --> 02:13:40.130
We also want to limit the maximum width

02:13:40.130 --> 02:13:43.710
in case somebody has
an ultra wide monitor.

02:13:43.710 --> 02:13:45.780
We don't want this to stretch all the way

02:13:45.780 --> 02:13:47.530
from the left to the right.

02:13:47.530 --> 02:13:52.230
So let's set a max with of 42rem

02:13:52.230 --> 02:13:54.080
because you know why not 42?

02:13:55.580 --> 02:13:58.480
And let's put a margin around of

02:14:00.660 --> 02:14:02.330
something like that to center it.

02:14:03.840 --> 02:14:05.800
And with that we can switch back

02:14:05.800 --> 02:14:10.170
and you can now see that
there's a much bigger margin.

02:14:10.170 --> 02:14:14.990
And if we inspect that
part of the application

02:14:14.990 --> 02:14:17.070
where we have our todos,

02:14:17.070 --> 02:14:21.420
you notice that there's no
margin at the top left and right.

02:14:21.420 --> 02:14:25.190
And if I was to change the size,

02:14:25.190 --> 02:14:29.680
this moves along and the
margin adjusts accordingly.

02:14:32.420 --> 02:14:33.253
Okay.

02:14:34.570 --> 02:14:37.030
One thing you do notice
is that Svelte created

02:14:39.242 --> 02:14:42.130
its own S dash class name.

02:14:42.130 --> 02:14:45.740
This is just so that we
Svelte can uniquely target

02:14:45.740 --> 02:14:50.740
that element with CSS so that
nothing we have to worry about

02:14:51.580 --> 02:14:53.880
it just happens under the hood for us.

02:14:56.970 --> 02:15:01.647
All right, the next thing we
want to do while we're here

02:15:02.590 --> 02:15:07.590
is we want to target that
new input field here.

02:15:09.860 --> 02:15:11.900
And as you see in the mock-up,

02:15:11.900 --> 02:15:15.110
it doesn't really have a background, so,

02:15:15.110 --> 02:15:17.330
or a border or things like that.

02:15:17.330 --> 02:15:22.330
So let's go back and
define the dot new class.

02:15:24.910 --> 02:15:28.330
And right now, Svelte is telling us that

02:15:28.330 --> 02:15:30.790
there's an unused CSS selector.

02:15:30.790 --> 02:15:35.790
And that's because we
specified a dot new class here,

02:15:37.280 --> 02:15:41.010
but we actually didn't use
it anywhere in the HTML.

02:15:41.010 --> 02:15:44.450
There's no new class anywhere applied.

02:15:44.450 --> 02:15:47.560
So this is a nice feature
of the Svelte compiler

02:15:47.560 --> 02:15:50.670
telling us that, Hey, you
have this element here,

02:15:50.670 --> 02:15:54.500
but it's not used so you
may as well delete it

02:15:54.500 --> 02:15:58.410
and save the browser from
downloading a few bytes

02:15:58.410 --> 02:16:01.320
by not having this definition.

02:16:01.320 --> 02:16:03.640
So that will come in handy,
you'll see the benefits of that

02:16:03.640 --> 02:16:06.690
once you start refactoring
your application,

02:16:08.667 --> 02:16:12.090
you may move, let's say
that form somewhere else

02:16:12.090 --> 02:16:14.590
and forget to include the CSS.

02:16:14.590 --> 02:16:17.750
Then you will get that warning
and you can move to CSS along

02:16:17.750 --> 02:16:21.510
with the form so that everything
continues to look the way

02:16:21.510 --> 02:16:22.510
you want it to look.

02:16:24.660 --> 02:16:29.270
So what do we want to do
for the input field here

02:16:29.270 --> 02:16:31.590
is first of all, just add
a bit of a margin here.

02:16:31.590 --> 02:16:34.100
They are a bit too close together,

02:16:34.100 --> 02:16:37.540
and that's easily done by first of all,

02:16:37.540 --> 02:16:42.540
going to our input field
for the new todo-item.

02:16:44.260 --> 02:16:48.340
And we have that wrapped within a form.

02:16:48.340 --> 02:16:51.760
So we can target that form
either with just form,

02:16:51.760 --> 02:16:54.323
or I like to be a bit more specific

02:16:54.323 --> 02:16:57.050
and do use a class name, and call it new.

02:16:57.050 --> 02:16:58.270
And now we still have an error,

02:16:58.270 --> 02:17:00.980
but it's only telling
us that please don't use

02:17:00.980 --> 02:17:04.310
empty rule sets, it recognized
that we have a class with new

02:17:04.310 --> 02:17:05.770
so that warning is gone.

02:17:07.010 --> 02:17:09.500
And then what we want to
say is just margin here,

02:17:11.800 --> 02:17:14.680
tiny a bit of margin at the bottom

02:17:15.780 --> 02:17:17.450
that applies nicely right there.

02:17:20.390 --> 02:17:21.223
Good.

02:17:22.790 --> 02:17:27.790
The other thing for the element,
the input element itself,

02:17:27.970 --> 02:17:32.840
we can target that as well
by creating another class

02:17:33.920 --> 02:17:37.230
so dot new, and then within that new form,

02:17:37.230 --> 02:17:40.740
we target the input element like this

02:17:43.040 --> 02:17:45.780
and that then applies CSS
for that specific element.

02:17:47.620 --> 02:17:49.890
What we want to do is a
couple things, first of all,

02:17:49.890 --> 02:17:51.880
it's a little bit bigger than the rest.

02:17:51.880 --> 02:17:56.790
So let's say font size 28 pixels,

02:17:56.790 --> 02:17:58.340
and then we want to stretch it,

02:18:01.510 --> 02:18:05.880
stretch it all the way and
give it some padding, such as,

02:18:10.760 --> 02:18:14.560
oops, sorry, 1em, 0.3,

02:18:17.190 --> 02:18:20.240
this is, the values here, you
know, I'm not making it up,

02:18:20.240 --> 02:18:22.810
that stuff that's in the default template.

02:18:22.810 --> 02:18:27.790
So since we're not really
focusing on the styling part,

02:18:28.640 --> 02:18:30.870
we can freely copy that and apply,

02:18:30.870 --> 02:18:33.810
so basically let me
add all that in one go.

02:18:34.650 --> 02:18:37.540
It's a bunch of things that apply only

02:18:37.540 --> 02:18:41.080
to that input field within the new class.

02:18:41.080 --> 02:18:45.570
So now this looks a lot closer
to what we eventually want it

02:18:45.570 --> 02:18:50.570
to look like, but we do notice
it has this border around it.

02:18:50.670 --> 02:18:54.700
And so do all the other
elements when you select them.

02:18:55.869 --> 02:18:57.790
We want to get rid of that,

02:18:59.267 --> 02:19:03.080
and we want to get rid of
that everywhere in this form.

02:19:03.080 --> 02:19:06.040
We could add the CSS to remove

02:19:06.040 --> 02:19:08.720
that border to the app.CSS file,

02:19:08.720 --> 02:19:12.430
and then it applies to
all the input fields

02:19:12.430 --> 02:19:13.930
across the entire application.

02:19:14.980 --> 02:19:16.830
For the purpose of this course,

02:19:16.830 --> 02:19:20.430
I want to introduce
you a concept in Svelte

02:19:21.310 --> 02:19:26.310
where we can target all the input fields

02:19:26.590 --> 02:19:30.320
in the current component and its children.

02:19:31.650 --> 02:19:34.380
So let's look at this
structure real quick.

02:19:34.380 --> 02:19:37.090
We have an input element here,

02:19:37.090 --> 02:19:40.410
and then we have input
elements within the todo-item.

02:19:40.410 --> 02:19:42.570
Remember under source, lib, todo-item.

02:19:43.760 --> 02:19:46.960
We have additional input fields.

02:19:46.960 --> 02:19:51.960
So if I were to come here
and let's say up here,

02:19:53.650 --> 02:19:56.520
well actually after the
new makes probably a sense.

02:19:56.520 --> 02:20:00.960
If I came here and I would just say, input

02:20:03.120 --> 02:20:05.751
the styling of that input class

02:20:05.751 --> 02:20:08.910
would only apply to this
input not the children

02:20:08.910 --> 02:20:11.510
because of the local scope
that I explained earlier.

02:20:12.790 --> 02:20:17.790
However, Svelte does allow us
to target this one input here

02:20:18.990 --> 02:20:22.010
and the ones in the components used.

02:20:22.010 --> 02:20:23.280
The way we do that is

02:20:24.880 --> 02:20:29.880
we limit it to that todos
class or that element here.

02:20:30.240 --> 02:20:33.440
So we say dot todos.

02:20:33.440 --> 02:20:34.920
And then instead of just input,

02:20:34.920 --> 02:20:39.190
we have to use a Svelte
specific configuration,

02:20:40.990 --> 02:20:45.310
so global input.

02:20:46.610 --> 02:20:51.610
Now that means target the dot todos class.

02:20:52.910 --> 02:20:54.880
So this element on line 35

02:20:56.580 --> 02:21:00.500
and then globally within
that, all the input fields,

02:21:00.500 --> 02:21:02.100
that's how we can apply styling

02:21:03.070 --> 02:21:06.290
to that input and to the
todo-item inputs inside.

02:21:06.290 --> 02:21:10.840
What do we want to do for these
items is remove that border.

02:21:10.840 --> 02:21:15.380
So border 1px solid, but
we make it transparent.

02:21:16.970 --> 02:21:21.110
And if we go to look at that,
that border disappeared,

02:21:21.110 --> 02:21:25.100
and if we click, we still get
the blue border around it.

02:21:25.980 --> 02:21:29.060
Same here, same here, but
the other border disappeared,

02:21:29.060 --> 02:21:32.610
we can fix that in the
same way as we did here,

02:21:32.610 --> 02:21:36.650
just that we have to use a pseudo element

02:21:38.164 --> 02:21:41.610
saying that target that
element when it's focused.

02:21:41.610 --> 02:21:45.370
So same thing to start with,

02:21:46.820 --> 02:21:49.440
but instead of targeting the input only,

02:21:49.440 --> 02:21:52.710
we want to target its focus visible state,

02:21:53.900 --> 02:21:57.970
and then the values we need there again,

02:21:57.970 --> 02:21:59.280
no need to type all that.

02:21:59.280 --> 02:22:01.430
This is not the main
purpose of the course.

02:22:02.620 --> 02:22:03.820
We'll add a bulk shadow,

02:22:04.902 --> 02:22:08.970
bit of border in red and outline none.

02:22:08.970 --> 02:22:11.960
If we look at that in the user interface,

02:22:11.960 --> 02:22:13.570
we now get this red border.

02:22:13.570 --> 02:22:16.370
So we know that's the element
we are currently typing in

02:22:17.270 --> 02:22:20.900
same here, automatically
applied to all these fields.

02:22:24.840 --> 02:22:28.510
With that, we styled everything we can

02:22:28.510 --> 02:22:32.150
in terms of the new input field.

02:22:32.150 --> 02:22:37.070
It's now time to move on and
style each individual item

02:22:37.070 --> 02:22:40.510
so that instead of having
these buttons and all that,

02:22:40.510 --> 02:22:42.740
it turns into this nice one row

02:22:44.351 --> 02:22:46.910
with the important stuff
that you see in the UI.

02:22:50.140 --> 02:22:53.620
For that, we switch to
source, lib, todo-item.

02:22:54.620 --> 02:22:56.300
And over here,

02:22:56.300 --> 02:23:01.100
this is where we can start
applying the exact same concept

02:23:01.100 --> 02:23:03.080
as we did in the index file,

02:23:04.160 --> 02:23:08.020
where we create a style element.

02:23:10.480 --> 02:23:12.480
And within the style element,

02:23:12.480 --> 02:23:15.530
we can create definitions and target

02:23:15.530 --> 02:23:17.690
the different elements that we have.

02:23:18.670 --> 02:23:20.260
So one note here,

02:23:21.660 --> 02:23:25.990
even though we define the
style element at the top,

02:23:25.990 --> 02:23:27.790
we could define it at the bottom

02:23:27.790 --> 02:23:31.600
if you prefer that,
there's no order required.

02:23:31.600 --> 02:23:33.590
Script, style, and HTML,

02:23:33.590 --> 02:23:37.150
you can order these three
within a Svelte component

02:23:37.150 --> 02:23:39.750
in whatever way you are
most comfortable with.

02:23:41.780 --> 02:23:42.700
Good.

02:23:42.700 --> 02:23:47.700
Let's do a couple of things
before we apply the styles.

02:23:48.510 --> 02:23:49.360
First of all,

02:23:50.420 --> 02:23:52.750
let's use some class names

02:23:52.750 --> 02:23:55.160
so that we can target individual elements.

02:23:56.040 --> 02:23:58.490
First things first, the wrapper div

02:23:58.490 --> 02:24:02.090
for an individual line
item, let's call this todo,

02:24:03.520 --> 02:24:06.020
and then we need to target that button

02:24:07.080 --> 02:24:11.780
so that we can turn this
Done/NotDone button here

02:24:11.780 --> 02:24:13.920
into a circle with a checkbox.

02:24:14.810 --> 02:24:16.250
So this is the button here,

02:24:17.160 --> 02:24:20.180
and we want to target that with a class.

02:24:20.180 --> 02:24:21.230
Let's call it toggle.

02:24:23.330 --> 02:24:26.140
Next thing we want to target the form

02:24:26.140 --> 02:24:30.090
that allows us to edit
a todo item and save it.

02:24:30.090 --> 02:24:34.840
So we can call this class,
stick to the same name

02:24:34.840 --> 02:24:37.730
as we already used for
other purposes, so text.

02:24:39.380 --> 02:24:41.950
And we also want to target the button,

02:24:41.950 --> 02:24:44.700
which will turn into a little save icon

02:24:44.700 --> 02:24:47.100
when somebody clicks in here.

02:24:49.230 --> 02:24:52.150
By default, if nobody
clicks, you see nothing.

02:24:52.150 --> 02:24:54.310
If somebody clicks on the text,

02:24:54.310 --> 02:24:56.950
then that save button
turns into a little icon.

02:24:58.110 --> 02:25:00.940
So we have to target that,
and the way we do that,

02:25:00.940 --> 02:25:04.680
as we add a class name
here and we call it save.

02:25:06.320 --> 02:25:09.260
Lastly, we need to
target the delete button.

02:25:09.260 --> 02:25:12.790
Let's give that a name, delete,

02:25:13.840 --> 02:25:18.470
and then let's start
adding the CSS required

02:25:18.470 --> 02:25:20.570
to turn this all into a nice line.

02:25:22.750 --> 02:25:24.190
All right, first things first,

02:25:24.190 --> 02:25:28.000
starting with the todo wrapper, so todo.

02:25:31.385 --> 02:25:33.720
For that, we use grid,

02:25:33.720 --> 02:25:37.580
but we could just equally use flex

02:25:37.580 --> 02:25:39.440
if you're more familiar with that,

02:25:39.440 --> 02:25:41.810
I'm going to paste all the CSS in here.

02:25:41.810 --> 02:25:45.380
Again, no need to go through
and describe all that.

02:25:45.380 --> 02:25:50.000
But it's basically just
applying a few styles

02:25:50.000 --> 02:25:53.000
so that things look like the
way they do in the mock-up.

02:25:53.000 --> 02:25:56.960
But there's great CSS courses out there

02:25:56.960 --> 02:25:59.630
that dive into a lot of
more detail around this.

02:26:01.110 --> 02:26:04.670
So have a look what we got,
everything is now aligned

02:26:04.670 --> 02:26:07.070
on the single line, so
that's a good first step.

02:26:08.990 --> 02:26:10.690
Next thing you want to do is focus

02:26:12.532 --> 02:26:14.470
on that button over here,

02:26:14.470 --> 02:26:18.507
and actually all of the
buttons, because we can,

02:26:19.920 --> 02:26:21.710
we can apply since they're the same styles

02:26:21.710 --> 02:26:23.540
to all of these three.

02:26:23.540 --> 02:26:28.540
So the way we can do that
is target the todo wrapper

02:26:29.550 --> 02:26:34.320
and then all buttons
went in, so all three.

02:26:35.910 --> 02:26:38.060
And for all these three,

02:26:38.060 --> 02:26:43.060
we want to specify just a couple of things

02:26:45.690 --> 02:26:50.150
width and hide the same
number so that we can apply

02:26:50.150 --> 02:26:54.160
a background position, 50
50%, which makes it a circle,

02:26:55.090 --> 02:26:57.120
remove some colors and make sure

02:26:57.120 --> 02:26:59.020
we don't repeat any of the background.

02:27:00.030 --> 02:27:04.210
If we have a look, that gray
background that the default

02:27:04.210 --> 02:27:06.690
button had is gone, we
still have the text.

02:27:06.690 --> 02:27:09.310
We'll drop that as soon as
we have the icons ready,

02:27:09.310 --> 02:27:11.430
and that will start to look a lot better.

02:27:13.520 --> 02:27:14.930
Buttons look good.

02:27:14.930 --> 02:27:16.970
The next thing we want to style are

02:27:16.970 --> 02:27:21.970
the input fields, so input.

02:27:23.910 --> 02:27:24.780
And for that,

02:27:25.940 --> 02:27:29.180
we only have really one input in this case

02:27:30.550 --> 02:27:35.550
that's visible, this one,
oops, the input here is hidden,

02:27:36.670 --> 02:27:40.513
but this input is visible,
so for that we use flex.

02:27:43.760 --> 02:27:48.570
And then we'll see why that is
as soon as we add the image.

02:27:50.220 --> 02:27:52.500
Not much changed here until I click,

02:27:52.500 --> 02:27:54.450
you can see that edit field eventually.

02:27:57.639 --> 02:28:01.840
Next up, let's go and
focus on the toggle button.

02:28:03.720 --> 02:28:08.610
So two ways I could say dot toggle,

02:28:10.390 --> 02:28:13.870
that would target that specific button.

02:28:13.870 --> 02:28:15.730
Alternatively, I can say,

02:28:15.730 --> 02:28:18.603
I want to target the button
with the class toggle,

02:28:18.603 --> 02:28:19.780
really up to you.

02:28:20.810 --> 02:28:22.870
We can do that and

02:28:25.980 --> 02:28:28.110
give that some CSS here.

02:28:29.600 --> 02:28:30.560
Oops, in here.

02:28:31.450 --> 02:28:32.650
Now, if we look at that,

02:28:34.660 --> 02:28:36.629
you can see not much changed yet

02:28:36.629 --> 02:28:38.940
because we don't have the image yet.

02:28:38.940 --> 02:28:40.670
We'll do that very shortly.

02:28:42.480 --> 02:28:47.480
Next thing we want to do is
target the text down here.

02:28:51.870 --> 02:28:53.500
So let's have a look.

02:28:56.790 --> 02:28:57.780
Oops, there we go.

02:28:58.810 --> 02:29:03.303
I want to say dot text
and the values for that,

02:29:08.090 --> 02:29:09.300
paste them here.

02:29:14.920 --> 02:29:18.730
Okay, now this is giving
us a lot more space

02:29:18.730 --> 02:29:23.730
because we are applying that
display flex and flex one.

02:29:24.000 --> 02:29:26.120
We now have a lot of space

02:29:26.120 --> 02:29:28.220
to type and edit our todo-item eventually.

02:29:29.660 --> 02:29:30.850
Good.

02:29:30.850 --> 02:29:35.850
Next up is the button
for the save element.

02:29:37.820 --> 02:29:42.820
So the right here, save button
let's replace that text.

02:29:44.750 --> 02:29:47.880
So for that, we remember
we have a safe class,

02:29:47.880 --> 02:29:52.563
so we can target it with
a definition like this.

02:29:54.860 --> 02:29:59.860
And the content, we can paste.

02:30:03.770 --> 02:30:05.570
I'll talk about it in a second here.

02:30:07.210 --> 02:30:11.140
So what do you see is that
opacity is zero by default,

02:30:11.140 --> 02:30:15.090
which means we don't see anything at the,

02:30:15.090 --> 02:30:19.170
by loading the page, and the
background image is just a SVG

02:30:20.110 --> 02:30:23.000
that is inlined within that component.

02:30:23.000 --> 02:30:26.680
The reason for that is that
this is such a small SVG

02:30:26.680 --> 02:30:30.450
with so very so few characters.

02:30:30.450 --> 02:30:33.030
It is probably more performant
to include that here rather

02:30:33.030 --> 02:30:38.030
than downloading an external
SVG file to display.

02:30:38.080 --> 02:30:41.160
So if we look at the page,

02:30:41.160 --> 02:30:45.820
you can see that we don't
see that save button anymore.

02:30:45.820 --> 02:30:49.920
If we inspected, it is
still here, dot save

02:30:51.721 --> 02:30:55.810
capacity is zero,

