04:58:06.100 --> 04:58:09.820
So now when somebody submits this form,

04:58:11.860 --> 04:58:14.130
we currently send,

04:58:14.130 --> 04:58:17.960
or we let the browser
send an HTTP post request

04:58:17.960 --> 04:58:22.660
to the endpoint that we
specified in the form here.

04:58:22.660 --> 04:58:24.540
And then that redirects us back.

04:58:25.930 --> 04:58:28.070
So what we really want to do
is we don't want the browser

04:58:28.070 --> 04:58:29.720
to take care of that.

04:58:29.720 --> 04:58:33.890
If JavaScript is enabled, we
want to tell the browser, Hey,

04:58:33.890 --> 04:58:34.970
thank you for your service,

04:58:34.970 --> 04:58:36.890
but we don't really need your help here.

04:58:36.890 --> 04:58:41.890
We can take care of sending
the content of this text field,

04:58:42.290 --> 04:58:46.220
to the API, process the API response,

04:58:46.220 --> 04:58:49.020
and then we'll update UI automatically.

04:58:49.020 --> 04:58:51.370
So we don't need to
reload the entire page.

04:58:52.440 --> 04:58:55.670
The way we do that is in our action,

04:58:55.670 --> 04:58:57.770
under source, lib, actions, form,

04:58:59.300 --> 04:59:02.630
we have access to the HTML form.

04:59:03.570 --> 04:59:07.220
So what we can do is we're
going to drop these console logs

04:59:07.220 --> 04:59:09.610
because we know they do
what they're supposed to do.

04:59:12.190 --> 04:59:16.240
So here we can say form.addEventListener,

04:59:17.390 --> 04:59:22.390
and we can listen to
the form's submit event.

04:59:25.420 --> 04:59:27.960
That's when the browser usually kicks in

04:59:27.960 --> 04:59:30.480
and does its post request to the API,

04:59:31.740 --> 04:59:36.690
and we need to give it a function
or a listener that will be

04:59:36.690 --> 04:59:40.870
executed when that event
happens, so let's call this

04:59:40.870 --> 04:59:45.770
handleSubmit, and then let's
create a function here,

04:59:48.020 --> 04:59:52.170
handleSubmit, and

04:59:54.649 --> 04:59:58.250
as I said, anything we do in our action,

04:59:58.250 --> 05:00:01.420
we have to undo it in
the destroy function.

05:00:01.420 --> 05:00:04.550
So when we move away to another
page, we want to say form

05:00:06.490 --> 05:00:11.250
removeEventListener, submit,

05:00:11.250 --> 05:00:13.630
and the event listener we want to remove

05:00:13.630 --> 05:00:15.080
is our handleSubmit function.

05:00:15.920 --> 05:00:16.753
So with that,

05:00:17.640 --> 05:00:21.090
we have the basic structure
in place of our action,

05:00:22.210 --> 05:00:26.390
where we add event listener, when the DOM,

05:00:26.390 --> 05:00:29.310
the element, it gets added
to the DOM and we remove it

05:00:29.310 --> 05:00:31.750
when the element gets
removed from the DOM.

05:00:33.550 --> 05:00:38.550
So what do we want to do
when a form gets submitted?

05:00:40.270 --> 05:00:43.050
The very first thing is we
want to tell the browser

05:00:43.960 --> 05:00:46.340
to not do the default behavior.

05:00:47.310 --> 05:00:49.900
As I said, the default behavior
is to send a post request

05:00:49.900 --> 05:00:52.800
to the endpoint that is
specified in the HTML.

05:00:53.860 --> 05:00:54.810
So in this case,

05:00:55.880 --> 05:01:00.030
send in a post request to
that todos item endpoint.

05:01:01.740 --> 05:01:02.750
Let's not do that.

05:01:02.750 --> 05:01:07.060
Now, because this is a regular
event listener that we added,

05:01:07.060 --> 05:01:11.670
we get a parameter passed in
here, which is called an event.

05:01:11.670 --> 05:01:13.830
And that's just of type event

05:01:13.830 --> 05:01:16.360
if we wanted to use TypeScript here.

05:01:16.360 --> 05:01:17.340
And on that event,

05:01:18.800 --> 05:01:22.960
we have a function that's
called prevent default

05:01:25.100 --> 05:01:27.360
that tells the browser, Hey,

05:01:27.360 --> 05:01:29.320
don't do your default behavior.

05:01:30.220 --> 05:01:31.460
I will take care of it.

05:01:32.300 --> 05:01:34.800
So let's see what that
does to our user interface.

05:01:36.130 --> 05:01:39.250
Clear out, network, clear.

05:01:40.630 --> 05:01:45.630
Alright, we add a second
item and we submit,

05:01:47.500 --> 05:01:50.810
I think that did not load,
let's try it one more time.

05:01:50.810 --> 05:01:55.810
Clear, add a third item and submit it.

05:01:57.800 --> 05:02:01.300
If you hit enter, nothing happens.

05:02:01.300 --> 05:02:02.780
And that's exactly what we want.

05:02:02.780 --> 05:02:05.580
And that's also what we
said, don't do anything.

05:02:05.580 --> 05:02:07.690
However because we told the browser

05:02:07.690 --> 05:02:10.170
not to do its default behavior,

05:02:10.170 --> 05:02:15.170
it is now up to us to make this
HTTP request to the backend.

05:02:17.640 --> 05:02:19.250
So let's have a look.

05:02:19.250 --> 05:02:21.230
Whenever we deal with an HTTP request,

05:02:21.230 --> 05:02:24.440
it's probably a good idea
to wrap it up in a try-catch

05:02:24.440 --> 05:02:27.920
because things could go wrong,
the API could be unavailable.

05:02:27.920 --> 05:02:32.060
There could be any other
errors that may occur.

05:02:33.160 --> 05:02:36.160
So if that happens, if we do
get an error for some reason,

05:02:37.000 --> 05:02:40.870
let's just say console.error
for the time being

05:02:42.010 --> 05:02:47.010
fetch error and eventually
specify what is wrong.

05:02:48.530 --> 05:02:49.830
We'll do that in a second.

05:02:50.990 --> 05:02:51.823
Okay.

05:02:52.790 --> 05:02:57.690
How do we make an HTTP
request from a website?

05:02:57.690 --> 05:03:00.280
Well, the solution to that is using fetch

05:03:02.040 --> 05:03:05.027
and we have to go to some
kind of endpoint here

05:03:09.248 --> 05:03:12.990
endpoint, alright, now
fetch is asynchronous,

05:03:12.990 --> 05:03:17.360
so we have to await it and
we will then get a response.

05:03:18.510 --> 05:03:19.930
Since we use now await,

05:03:19.930 --> 05:03:23.640
we have to make the handler
asynchronous, we'll do async.

05:03:24.670 --> 05:03:25.503
All right.

05:03:27.260 --> 05:03:30.410
We'll figure it out, that
endpoint in just a second.

05:03:30.410 --> 05:03:33.990
But for now, we now have a response that

05:03:35.630 --> 05:03:37.570
if something does go wrong,

05:03:37.570 --> 05:03:41.940
then we can do something with it.

05:03:42.830 --> 05:03:47.830
So the let's change this here
to could not submit the form.

05:03:50.380 --> 05:03:54.700
And the error we get is
whatever error we have.

05:03:56.420 --> 05:03:59.870
All right, first things first,

05:03:59.870 --> 05:04:04.870
how do we figure out what's
the endpoint of our form?

05:04:04.950 --> 05:04:08.260
If we look at the index file, the endpoint

05:04:08.260 --> 05:04:12.480
we want to use this value
within the action property.

05:04:12.480 --> 05:04:17.200
Remember that our action we
are defining here is generic

05:04:17.200 --> 05:04:22.110
so that we can use it for
any form on the application.

05:04:22.110 --> 05:04:25.380
We don't want this to be very
specific to just one form.

05:04:26.300 --> 05:04:30.220
And the good thing is that if
we look at the form elements

05:04:31.480 --> 05:04:35.550
that we defined under
source lib todo-item,

05:04:36.440 --> 05:04:41.020
and we go down all these forms,

05:04:41.020 --> 05:04:43.390
they specify the action attribute

05:04:43.390 --> 05:04:47.500
that we want to use for our HTTP request.

05:04:47.500 --> 05:04:50.930
So the good news here is that
we do get a form passed in

05:04:50.930 --> 05:04:54.050
as an attribute here, or as a parameter.

05:04:55.480 --> 05:04:57.250
So let's have a look.

05:04:57.250 --> 05:05:00.550
What if we look at
what's inside that form,

05:05:02.677 --> 05:05:07.273
do we have, ah, we have
the action property.

05:05:08.120 --> 05:05:10.480
Okay, that's exactly what we want.

05:05:10.480 --> 05:05:14.060
We want to use that action
as the URL for our request.

05:05:14.950 --> 05:05:17.010
So this is a good start, now,

05:05:18.110 --> 05:05:21.650
if we don't specify any more
parameters for the fetch call,

05:05:21.650 --> 05:05:25.970
then the default request
method would be get.

05:05:26.810 --> 05:05:31.550
But as you know, we want
this to be post like here,

05:05:32.610 --> 05:05:36.150
so we can just like access
the action attribute,

05:05:36.150 --> 05:05:38.710
we can access the method as well.

05:05:38.710 --> 05:05:42.260
So we can pass in a second
argument to the fetch function

05:05:43.130 --> 05:05:47.300
and we call this method and the
value is whatever we specify

05:05:47.300 --> 05:05:49.730
in the form method attribute.

05:05:52.156 --> 05:05:55.630
Alrighty, there's one
more thing we need to do.

05:05:55.630 --> 05:06:00.120
So in the case of creating a todo-item

05:06:01.534 --> 05:06:06.400
or updating an item, we
need to send the fields,

05:06:06.400 --> 05:06:08.850
the input fields that we
specify within the form.

05:06:09.800 --> 05:06:12.830
And in order to do that,

05:06:12.830 --> 05:06:16.630
we can leverage a pretty nifty feature.

05:06:17.720 --> 05:06:19.630
So let's specify,

05:06:20.780 --> 05:06:24.030
let's say a body and
the way we can do this,

05:06:24.030 --> 05:06:28.720
we can use new FormData, pass in the form.

05:06:31.280 --> 05:06:33.700
Does this look familiar, FormData?

05:06:34.570 --> 05:06:36.590
In fact, I still have it open as a tab.

05:06:36.590 --> 05:06:40.800
We looked at that earlier on
the Mozilla developer page.

05:06:40.800 --> 05:06:43.340
And if we switch over to the roots todos,

05:06:44.550 --> 05:06:49.550
the UID or the index, so index.post here,

05:06:50.950 --> 05:06:53.010
remember we specified the FormData

05:06:53.010 --> 05:06:54.520
that is being sent as a body.

05:06:55.720 --> 05:06:57.710
This is exactly what we're doing here.

05:06:57.710 --> 05:07:02.710
We create the body as a new
instance of the FormData.

05:07:02.960 --> 05:07:05.980
And now in our request,

05:07:05.980 --> 05:07:09.290
we can specify a body attribute.

05:07:10.490 --> 05:07:13.790
And since it's the same
as the variable name,

05:07:13.790 --> 05:07:16.680
we can just leave it like
that instead of doing this

05:07:17.650 --> 05:07:19.390
short form is just using body.

05:07:20.580 --> 05:07:23.620
So now we are sending a request

05:07:24.740 --> 05:07:28.460
to the action with the
correct method and the body,

05:07:28.460 --> 05:07:30.260
but there's one more thing we can do

05:07:31.360 --> 05:07:36.360
that is, we can specify

05:07:37.400 --> 05:07:41.860
that we accept a JSON response.

05:07:41.860 --> 05:07:44.510
So let's have a look, we
can pass in headers as well.

05:07:46.280 --> 05:07:51.130
So headers, and what
do we say here is, Hey,

05:07:52.690 --> 05:07:56.200
we accept application/JSON,

05:07:59.340 --> 05:08:01.670
and this will come in handy
in just a second and I'll show

05:08:01.670 --> 05:08:06.670
you why, for now, let's just,
you know, make this request,

05:08:07.100 --> 05:08:09.550
we get a response, and then we want

05:08:09.550 --> 05:08:11.960
to do something with that response.

05:08:11.960 --> 05:08:16.960
So let's go here and say
that if the response is ok,

05:08:17.990 --> 05:08:22.990
then we want to console log API response.

05:08:28.040 --> 05:08:32.200
And for the time being, we just say await.

05:08:35.200 --> 05:08:37.340
So what is happening here?

05:08:38.230 --> 05:08:41.700
We basically just pick the
JSON response that we get

05:08:41.700 --> 05:08:46.700
from the server and what just
print it into the console.

05:08:47.730 --> 05:08:51.410
If something goes wrong, let's say else,

05:08:52.440 --> 05:08:57.440
we want console error, fetch error.

05:08:58.210 --> 05:09:03.210
And we will just take whatever
the response text attribute

05:09:03.500 --> 05:09:05.350
is like, if something happened there,

05:09:06.640 --> 05:09:09.080
then the response will
contain some information

05:09:09.080 --> 05:09:10.510
of what went wrong.

05:09:10.510 --> 05:09:12.480
And we can leverage that

05:09:12.480 --> 05:09:16.610
to display an error message
in the console for now.

05:09:18.730 --> 05:09:23.580
Okay, so we did a lot of stuff here.

05:09:23.580 --> 05:09:26.420
Now, what do we want to go check is

05:09:28.110 --> 05:09:30.190
what happens when we submit that request.

05:09:31.980 --> 05:09:36.440
Let's go back here and
let's reload the page.

05:09:38.460 --> 05:09:39.293
Clear all that.

05:09:40.660 --> 05:09:44.650
Let's go and create a third
item, and we submit it.

05:09:46.640 --> 05:09:48.290
Interesting, look at that.

05:09:49.390 --> 05:09:53.520
First of all, it didn't do any
redirect, which is, you know,

05:09:53.520 --> 05:09:58.483
kind of good news, however,
it tried to, see this?

05:10:01.760 --> 05:10:06.760
So a post request 303
is the response code.

05:10:07.130 --> 05:10:10.620
And it tried to redirect
back to that index page.

05:10:10.620 --> 05:10:15.310
So why is that, well, because
we configured it that way,

05:10:15.310 --> 05:10:17.830
obviously, there's a POST request.

05:10:17.830 --> 05:10:22.830
And if we go to our API
source routes todos API,

05:10:24.820 --> 05:10:29.820
you recall that we say that if
the request method is not get

05:10:30.510 --> 05:10:35.510
then redirect to the index
page, ha that's a problem.

05:10:37.710 --> 05:10:39.850
We only want to do that redirect

05:10:39.850 --> 05:10:44.770
when the browser submits
the form on its own,

05:10:44.770 --> 05:10:46.540
so when JavaScript is disabled.

05:10:47.600 --> 05:10:52.090
If JavaScript is enabled
and we submit the form

05:10:53.030 --> 05:10:56.860
through our action right
here, in that case,

05:10:56.860 --> 05:11:00.030
we don't want to do a redirect
and instead we just want

05:11:00.030 --> 05:11:05.030
to return the response
and then we parse it here

05:11:05.550 --> 05:11:07.980
and we print the value, right?

05:11:07.980 --> 05:11:09.935
All right so let's have a look

05:11:09.935 --> 05:11:12.994
at how we can go about that and make sure

05:11:12.994 --> 05:11:16.370
we can differentiate whether
the browser submitted

05:11:16.370 --> 05:11:18.980
the request or whether we did.

05:11:20.010 --> 05:11:22.910
Now, I already did a little
bit of work here upfront.

05:11:22.910 --> 05:11:27.550
I added a new header that
says accept application/json.

05:11:28.460 --> 05:11:31.660
The browser doesn't do that
when it submits the form,

05:11:32.510 --> 05:11:36.710
the value for that is
different in that case.

05:11:36.710 --> 05:11:40.030
But because we are
specifying application/json

05:11:40.030 --> 05:11:42.790
by ourselves, when JavaScript is enabled,

05:11:42.790 --> 05:11:47.370
we can leverage that in our
API file and enhance our

05:11:47.370 --> 05:11:52.180
if statement so that we are not
only looking for the request

05:11:52.180 --> 05:11:57.180
method, but it also checking
the header that we specified.

05:11:58.520 --> 05:12:03.520
So here we say and request headers accept,

05:12:05.480 --> 05:12:07.570
that's the one we specified

05:12:08.540 --> 05:12:10.720
is not equals to application/json.

05:12:14.410 --> 05:12:17.920
Probably a good time to move
that to a new line, okay,

05:12:20.410 --> 05:12:25.410
with that, we now have the check

05:12:25.460 --> 05:12:27.880
so they shouldn't do a redirect anymore

05:12:27.880 --> 05:12:30.630
when we sent the request,
let's see if that's the case.

05:12:31.960 --> 05:12:36.240
Because we changed our API
file, the server reloaded,

05:12:36.240 --> 05:12:37.600
and it cleared our items.

05:12:38.500 --> 05:12:41.660
That's okay, we'll just add them again.

05:12:41.660 --> 05:12:42.670
So we add a first,

05:12:44.940 --> 05:12:47.620
that, okay, hang on, let's
clear this, it will be easier.

05:12:47.620 --> 05:12:48.620
We'll do that again.

05:12:49.810 --> 05:12:52.280
We'll add a second.

05:12:54.330 --> 05:12:56.800
And now look at that,
we only have one request

05:12:59.420 --> 05:13:04.420
and if we move things around here a bit,

05:13:06.250 --> 05:13:10.130
you can see it's a post
request, the status code is 201,

05:13:11.090 --> 05:13:15.480
and you can see that the
payload was text second,

05:13:15.480 --> 05:13:16.690
that's what we created.

05:13:17.920 --> 05:13:21.510
And the response that we
got back from the server

05:13:23.031 --> 05:13:26.300
is a full todo-item,
including the created at,

05:13:27.200 --> 05:13:29.400
the UID, the text, done false.

05:13:30.290 --> 05:13:33.370
This is fascinating, this
is all we really need.

05:13:34.220 --> 05:13:37.870
And in fact, all we need to do now is

05:13:37.870 --> 05:13:41.840
update the user interface
to make sure we showed

05:13:41.840 --> 05:13:44.050
that item in the UI.

05:13:44.050 --> 05:13:46.430
At the moment, if I were
to refresh the page,

05:13:47.450 --> 05:13:49.810
you can see that our two items are here.

05:13:49.810 --> 05:13:51.670
That's because the GET request

05:13:51.670 --> 05:13:54.850
fetches everything from
the server and displays it.

05:13:54.850 --> 05:13:56.620
But we want to do that dynamically.

05:13:57.580 --> 05:13:58.670
And the way we can do that

05:13:58.670 --> 05:14:01.260
is because we're dealing with JavaScript,

05:14:01.260 --> 05:14:02.970
we also can modify that list

05:14:04.942 --> 05:14:07.290
and do so in a pretty dynamic way.

05:14:08.680 --> 05:14:10.130
Let's look at how we do that.

05:14:15.280 --> 05:14:19.530
At the moment, when we
have a successful result,

05:14:19.530 --> 05:14:23.950
we just print API response
out to the console.

05:14:23.950 --> 05:14:25.220
So if we look at that,

05:14:27.060 --> 05:14:29.030
I disappeared because I reloaded the page.

05:14:29.030 --> 05:14:31.280
We can do it again, if I add a third item,

05:14:32.170 --> 05:14:34.060
we can see the API response here.

05:14:35.150 --> 05:14:38.780
Now let's take that information

05:14:38.780 --> 05:14:41.100
and update the user interface.

05:14:42.530 --> 05:14:47.530
And the way we do that is,
instead of just printing,

05:14:47.680 --> 05:14:52.620
we have to update the user
interface in the index file.

05:14:53.820 --> 05:14:54.720
Let's have a look.

05:14:57.990 --> 05:15:00.680
In the index file, we
have that enhance action.

05:15:01.940 --> 05:15:06.940
So somehow when the request
is made to the server

05:15:08.200 --> 05:15:11.730
from our action, and we
get the response back,

05:15:11.730 --> 05:15:13.710
at this point right here,

05:15:13.710 --> 05:15:18.530
we need to update the user interface here.

05:15:18.530 --> 05:15:21.250
So the number of todos
that we have in this array,

05:15:21.250 --> 05:15:23.660
we need to push in our new item

05:15:23.660 --> 05:15:25.360
that the server sent back to us.

05:15:26.600 --> 05:15:30.260
With that, we can then
let Svelte to do the rest

05:15:30.260 --> 05:15:33.550
of the matching and
just rerender that array

05:15:33.550 --> 05:15:35.870
and display a new todo-item.

05:15:35.870 --> 05:15:40.870
But how do we access that
array that we get in as a prop?

05:15:44.900 --> 05:15:46.500
There's some good news.

05:15:46.500 --> 05:15:51.500
And the good news is that we
can pass additional parameters

05:15:52.270 --> 05:15:53.700
to our action.

05:15:53.700 --> 05:15:55.930
The form is given to us by Svelte,

05:15:55.930 --> 05:16:00.780
but we can add our own parameters as well.

05:16:00.780 --> 05:16:03.490
And that's exactly what
we're going to do, so

05:16:06.160 --> 05:16:08.660
in order to make this more
flexible going forward,

05:16:09.820 --> 05:16:13.570
let's add an object as
an additional parameter.

05:16:13.570 --> 05:16:16.920
And what we will pass in is a function

05:16:16.920 --> 05:16:18.450
that we named result.

05:16:21.080 --> 05:16:22.590
Why do we do that?

05:16:22.590 --> 05:16:25.330
Because this enhanced action will be used

05:16:25.330 --> 05:16:27.260
across multiple forms.

05:16:27.260 --> 05:16:32.260
The behavior of the success
case is going to be different.

05:16:32.290 --> 05:16:34.990
When we add an item, we
want to add it to a list.

05:16:34.990 --> 05:16:38.200
When we edit it, we
want to update an item.

05:16:38.200 --> 05:16:40.510
And when we delete a todo-item,

05:16:40.510 --> 05:16:42.890
we want to remove it
from our list in the UI.

05:16:43.840 --> 05:16:45.510
So because of that,

05:16:45.510 --> 05:16:47.730
we basically pass in that function,

05:16:47.730 --> 05:16:50.670
again, you could call
it whatever you want,

05:16:50.670 --> 05:16:55.450
