And the package name is prisma.

05:53:59.430 --> 05:54:02.760
If we run that, it gets it all installed.

05:54:03.600 --> 05:54:08.600
And now we can access the
node_modules bin directory,

05:54:09.730 --> 05:54:13.070
where we have a Prisma executable.

05:54:13.070 --> 05:54:17.898
And this is how we access
that new package's CLI,

05:54:17.898 --> 05:54:19.520
that's a lot to type.

05:54:19.520 --> 05:54:24.000
So the alternative to
that path is to use npx

05:54:25.577 --> 05:54:28.680
and then the name of the
CLI that we want to call.

05:54:29.860 --> 05:54:31.120
Before we go any further,

05:54:32.300 --> 05:54:37.100
I want to show you what
Prisma Studio is all about.

05:54:37.100 --> 05:54:41.280
Prisma Studio is a web
application that connects

05:54:41.280 --> 05:54:45.740
to our database and we can
then inspect the tables

05:54:45.740 --> 05:54:48.500
and the rows and the
columns of our database.

05:54:48.500 --> 05:54:52.790
So we can validate that
the information we send

05:54:53.780 --> 05:54:56.625
from the API to the
database gets correctly

05:54:56.625 --> 05:54:59.700
persisted or updated, depending
on the action we perform.

05:54:59.700 --> 05:55:02.140
So in order to start the studio,

05:55:02.140 --> 05:55:04.900
we can see npx prisma studio

05:55:06.940 --> 05:55:08.680
and start that by hitting enter.

05:55:08.680 --> 05:55:11.790
At this time, this will
fail and we will see why.

05:55:13.770 --> 05:55:17.030
Error, could not find
a schema.prisma file.

05:55:17.970 --> 05:55:22.970
So the schema.prisma file
contains the necessary information

05:55:24.370 --> 05:55:27.710
for Prisma to understand
everything it needs

05:55:27.710 --> 05:55:29.650
to know about our database,

05:55:29.650 --> 05:55:32.910
such as what's the database provider,

05:55:32.910 --> 05:55:35.260
what's the URL to connect to it,

05:55:35.260 --> 05:55:39.610
and what does the database look
like in terms of its schema,

05:55:39.610 --> 05:55:41.660
what tables are available,

05:55:41.660 --> 05:55:44.070
what columns are within these tables,

05:55:44.070 --> 05:55:46.370
and what kind of types are these columns.

05:55:47.320 --> 05:55:49.420
So if we read the error message carefully,

05:55:51.660 --> 05:55:55.950
we noticed that at the bottom,

05:55:55.950 --> 05:55:59.890
it tells us that the
default location of the file

05:56:01.262 --> 05:56:04.280
is a Prisma directory slash schema.prisma.

05:56:04.280 --> 05:56:08.190
So if we take that path, we copy it.,

05:56:08.190 --> 05:56:11.390
and in our root, in the editor,

05:56:11.390 --> 05:56:15.860
we create a new file for
Prisma slash schema.prisma

05:56:17.650 --> 05:56:20.410
that generates the file that we need.

05:56:20.410 --> 05:56:24.320
And now we can go ahead
and start editing it.

05:56:24.320 --> 05:56:28.180
You saw I tried an
autocomplete, that didn't work.

05:56:28.180 --> 05:56:31.460
The reason for that is that
we don't have a VS Code

05:56:31.460 --> 05:56:36.250
extension installed that
understands this file.

05:56:36.250 --> 05:56:39.590
So we can head over to VS Code extensions

05:56:40.870 --> 05:56:42.980
and search for Prisma.

05:56:44.300 --> 05:56:46.670
There's an insider and a stable version.

05:56:46.670 --> 05:56:49.760
I prefer to stable
version, so we click on it.

05:56:49.760 --> 05:56:53.800
And over here, you remember
that from the Svelte extension

05:56:53.800 --> 05:56:55.680
that we installed earlier

05:56:55.680 --> 05:56:57.980
under manage, if you
say, add to Gitpod YAML,

05:56:58.860 --> 05:57:03.630
that will ensure that everybody
who opens this project

05:57:03.630 --> 05:57:06.190
on a gitpod workspace will have

05:57:06.190 --> 05:57:10.310
the Prisma extension installed by default.

05:57:10.310 --> 05:57:13.232
Just to show you it ended up over here

05:57:13.232 --> 05:57:17.280
under VS Code extensions
within the Gitpod YAML file.

05:57:18.260 --> 05:57:19.750
Currently, we don't have it installed,

05:57:19.750 --> 05:57:21.610
I just added it to the file.

05:57:21.610 --> 05:57:24.980
So we can either start a
new workspace with that new

05:57:24.980 --> 05:57:27.420
configuration file or easier way

05:57:27.420 --> 05:57:30.220
is to hit the quick fix link

05:57:30.220 --> 05:57:32.590
and say install prisma extension.

05:57:34.790 --> 05:57:38.500
That doesn't take very long
and once it's installed,

05:57:38.500 --> 05:57:41.270
then the line will disappear.

05:57:41.270 --> 05:57:44.330
And now if we go back to our schema file

05:57:44.330 --> 05:57:46.970
and we use autocomplete, control space,

05:57:47.860 --> 05:57:52.730
we now have access to the
properties that we need to define.

05:57:53.870 --> 05:57:55.620
One more thing I'd like to do

05:57:55.620 --> 05:58:00.460
for future us and our
colleagues, in a file like this,

05:58:00.460 --> 05:58:02.700
where it may not be obvious

05:58:02.700 --> 05:58:05.180
where we can find more
information about it.

05:58:05.180 --> 05:58:06.840
I'd like to just add a comment,

05:58:06.840 --> 05:58:09.960
learn more about this file in the docs

05:58:10.890 --> 05:58:15.890
and the URL for that is HTTPs
and pris.ly/d/prisma.schema,

05:58:22.490 --> 05:58:27.290
quickly double check that
there's no spelling mistakes.

05:58:27.290 --> 05:58:30.240
That looks good, all the
information you need in here.

05:58:30.240 --> 05:58:31.950
As I said, the Prisma team

05:58:31.950 --> 05:58:34.680
did a fantastic job
documenting everything.

05:58:34.680 --> 05:58:35.660
So we'll drop that.

05:58:36.840 --> 05:58:40.860
And what we can do now is

05:58:42.590 --> 05:58:46.250
define our datasource, datasource DB,

05:58:46.250 --> 05:58:49.530
and then the values inside,
lets use autocomplete,

05:58:49.530 --> 05:58:54.530
it asks for the provider
and the provider we want

05:58:54.680 --> 05:58:56.680
is in our case Postgres.

05:58:57.520 --> 05:58:59.470
So we have other options here as well.

05:58:59.470 --> 05:59:02.280
We already know we install
the Postgres database,

05:59:02.280 --> 05:59:06.510
so we'll choose Postgres
and still something missing,

05:59:06.510 --> 05:59:07.920
which is the URL.

05:59:11.350 --> 05:59:16.350
The URL is depending on your environment,

05:59:16.850 --> 05:59:18.960
so generally speaking,

05:59:18.960 --> 05:59:23.960
the format of a Postgres URL

05:59:24.690 --> 05:59:29.060
is Postgres colon slash slash,

05:59:29.060 --> 05:59:33.460
and then the username that
you created in your database.

05:59:33.460 --> 05:59:37.400
In our case, because we
use the Gitpod Docker image

05:59:37.400 --> 05:59:40.530
as our foundation for the
developer environment,

05:59:40.530 --> 05:59:45.280
I know the username is Gitpod,
and then @ the host name,

05:59:45.280 --> 05:59:47.260
so localhost in our case,

05:59:47.260 --> 05:59:50.230
slash the name of the
database we want to create.

05:59:51.990 --> 05:59:54.800
So this in theory works just fine

05:59:54.800 --> 05:59:58.330
in our local development
environment or here on Gitpod.

05:59:58.330 --> 06:00:00.520
But when we deploy to production,

06:00:00.520 --> 06:00:04.160
this is no longer going to
work because that path is going

06:00:04.160 --> 06:00:06.280
to be something completely different

06:00:06.280 --> 06:00:08.870
that we need to set dynamically.

06:00:08.870 --> 06:00:13.815
So luckily the Prisma
schema file allows us

06:00:13.815 --> 06:00:16.480
to use something called ENV.

06:00:16.480 --> 06:00:19.110
So this is a way for us to use

06:00:19.110 --> 06:00:23.240
an environment variable
instead of a hard-coded string.

06:00:23.240 --> 06:00:27.120
And what we want to do
here is DATABASE_URL.

06:00:31.230 --> 06:00:36.230
So with that, we now have
to set the DATABASE_URL

06:00:37.800 --> 06:00:40.400
in our development environment.

06:00:40.400 --> 06:00:44.180
One way to do that is as you
also see in the docs here,

06:00:44.180 --> 06:00:48.820
we could create a .env file at the root

06:00:48.820 --> 06:00:52.570
and configure the
environment variable there.

06:00:52.570 --> 06:00:56.140
Another way since we're
using Gitpod is we can go

06:00:57.770 --> 06:01:01.413
to the Gitpod YAML file, and in our tasks,

06:01:03.090 --> 06:01:04.590
we currently have one terminal

06:01:05.490 --> 06:01:07.930
indicated by the one dash here.

06:01:07.930 --> 06:01:12.340
So here we can just add env
and then say DATABASE_URL.

06:01:15.040 --> 06:01:19.403
And the value of that is
that Postgres localhost URL

06:01:20.680 --> 06:01:21.830
that we defined before.

06:01:22.680 --> 06:01:25.670
So now we know that when
we start a new workspace,

06:01:25.670 --> 06:01:28.810
the terminal with the server

06:01:28.810 --> 06:01:30.640
will have that DATABASE_URL available,

06:01:30.640 --> 06:01:32.740
so that's pretty neat,

06:01:32.740 --> 06:01:34.970
that's all we need for the time being.

06:01:36.840 --> 06:01:39.720
Let's go back to the Prisma schema,

06:01:39.720 --> 06:01:41.080
so the schema.prisma file,

06:01:42.000 --> 06:01:44.270
we have to configure a
few more things here.

06:01:44.270 --> 06:01:47.940
The next thing is we need a generator.

06:01:47.940 --> 06:01:51.020
As I mentioned briefly,
when I introduced Prisma,

06:01:51.950 --> 06:01:54.330
based on our database schema,

06:01:55.360 --> 06:01:59.950
Prisma will generate a JavaScript
slash TypeScript client

06:01:59.950 --> 06:02:03.170
that we can load into our API

06:02:03.170 --> 06:02:05.500
and then interact with the database.

06:02:05.500 --> 06:02:10.420
So what'd we do here, we specify
a few parameters for that.

06:02:10.420 --> 06:02:14.890
So we do generator client and

06:02:14.890 --> 06:02:16.850
bunch of things it's asking for,

06:02:16.850 --> 06:02:18.600
we'll start with the provider.

06:02:19.940 --> 06:02:24.030
And the value is, there's
only one prisma-client-js.

06:02:25.900 --> 06:02:30.900
The other thing we want to do
is specify the binaryTargets.

06:02:32.880 --> 06:02:36.900
And that is important because
as you see in the description,

06:02:36.900 --> 06:02:39.050
it specifies the operating system on which

06:02:39.050 --> 06:02:42.980
the Prisma client will run to
ensure binary compatibility

06:02:42.980 --> 06:02:44.110
of the query engine.

06:02:46.150 --> 06:02:50.410
A lot of text, what it
really means is, right now,

06:02:50.410 --> 06:02:52.840
the Gitpod developer environment uses

06:02:52.840 --> 06:02:55.720
a Ubuntu operating system.

06:02:55.720 --> 06:02:57.910
If you follow along in
your local computer,

06:02:57.910 --> 06:02:59.230
may you may use Windows,

06:02:59.230 --> 06:03:02.790
or you may use MacOS as your environment.

06:03:03.640 --> 06:03:07.420
What we're doing here
is we're just indicating

06:03:08.830 --> 06:03:11.480
that the binaryTargets we
want the client to generate

06:03:13.155 --> 06:03:14.580
is whatever, I'll show you,

06:03:16.374 --> 06:03:18.740
whatever the native environment is.

06:03:18.740 --> 06:03:23.230
That's all the documented,
but native means in this case,

06:03:23.230 --> 06:03:27.380
it will generate it for Ubuntu,
when we deploy to Vercel,

06:03:27.380 --> 06:03:29.850
it will generate it for
whatever operating system

06:03:29.850 --> 06:03:32.180
Vercel uses to run our API.

06:03:33.120 --> 06:03:35.090
So that gives us the peace of mind,

06:03:35.090 --> 06:03:36.940
we don't have to worry about anymore.

06:03:37.950 --> 06:03:41.000
Lastly, and arguably, most importantly,

06:03:41.950 --> 06:03:45.020
we have to define a database schema.

06:03:46.410 --> 06:03:50.490
And what that means is if we
look at the API just real quick

06:03:50.490 --> 06:03:54.400
source, routes, todos _API,

06:03:55.970 --> 06:03:59.710
remember that todos
array that we specified?

06:04:00.750 --> 06:04:02.670
And at the moment, this is just

06:04:02.670 --> 06:04:05.280
an array, a variable living in memory

06:04:05.280 --> 06:04:08.580
that needs to move to the
database, so in the database,

06:04:08.580 --> 06:04:12.930
we need a table that can
hold all the todo-items

06:04:12.930 --> 06:04:15.300
that we add to our web application.

06:04:16.340 --> 06:04:21.290
So we are lucky in so far that we have

06:04:21.290 --> 06:04:24.140
already a Todo TypeScript definition.

06:04:25.020 --> 06:04:28.660
So let's head over there
and look at what we defined.

06:04:29.620 --> 06:04:33.593
That definition is under src global.d.ts.

06:04:35.730 --> 06:04:40.730
And you can see, we have a
UID, created at, a text a done.

06:04:41.430 --> 06:04:42.450
So let's copy that

06:04:44.080 --> 06:04:47.030
so that we make sure we don't
forget any of the properties.

06:04:48.220 --> 06:04:49.920
And we move back to the schema.

06:04:50.920 --> 06:04:53.690
And then what we define
here is called a model

06:04:54.860 --> 06:04:56.900
and the name of the model,

06:04:56.900 --> 06:05:00.900
you can roughly think of that
as the table name as well.

06:05:00.900 --> 06:05:02.310
So that would be todo,

06:05:04.800 --> 06:05:07.800
and within that, we can
specify the properties.

06:05:07.800 --> 06:05:12.800
So for now, let's just paste
our TypeScript definitions.

06:05:13.980 --> 06:05:17.190
And now we just have to update the syntax

06:05:17.190 --> 06:05:21.150
to match the syntax used
within our schema Prisma file.

06:05:21.150 --> 06:05:26.150
That's pretty straightforward,
so we do it all in one go.

06:05:26.890 --> 06:05:30.070
We remove the colons,

06:05:30.980 --> 06:05:33.010
replace them with a space.

06:05:34.050 --> 06:05:36.720
And then the next thing is that

06:05:36.720 --> 06:05:40.470
String is a capital S, so is it here,

06:05:40.470 --> 06:05:45.470
so is Boolean and date is
actually more specific a DateTime

06:05:47.390 --> 06:05:49.780
and we also need to drop
all these semi-colons.

06:05:51.310 --> 06:05:55.850
And with that, we are almost ready to go.

06:05:55.850 --> 06:06:00.800
It's still complaining about an error.

06:06:00.800 --> 06:06:04.090
And it's saying that each
model must have at least one

06:06:04.090 --> 06:06:08.330
unique criteria that has only
required fields, blah, blah,

06:06:08.330 --> 06:06:10.430
either mark or field ID.

06:06:10.430 --> 06:06:14.380
Now, what that means
is remember in the API

06:06:17.540 --> 06:06:19.240
source, routes, todos, index.json.

06:06:20.910 --> 06:06:23.160
Remember we created that UID

06:06:23.160 --> 06:06:25.810
and we used Date.now, which
as I mentioned earlier,

06:06:25.810 --> 06:06:28.180
never ever do that in a
production environment.

06:06:29.020 --> 06:06:32.240
And we even left a note saying that,

06:06:32.240 --> 06:06:34.960
replace that with the
UID from the database,

06:06:35.800 --> 06:06:38.390
nice spelling mistake, okay, so

06:06:39.910 --> 06:06:43.910
at this point we have
access to the database.

06:06:43.910 --> 06:06:46.990
We are literally defining
the schema right now,

06:06:46.990 --> 06:06:49.390
so we can now drop this line.

06:06:49.390 --> 06:06:51.960
We no longer need to create it.

06:06:53.060 --> 06:06:56.780
And what we do instead is we remove that,

06:06:58.180 --> 06:07:01.017
and in our schema, we
can tell the database

06:07:01.017 --> 06:07:05.000
to use that UID field as the unique ID.

06:07:06.310 --> 06:07:10.960
And in Prisma, we can
use the @id for that,

06:07:10.960 --> 06:07:13.760
and also we want to
give it a default value.

06:07:15.120 --> 06:07:17.620
And the default value,
we can use a function

06:07:19.078 --> 06:07:21.580
to create that global unique identifier

06:07:21.580 --> 06:07:25.910
based on the cuid spec, so I'll use that.

06:07:25.910 --> 06:07:27.000
And with it,

06:07:27.000 --> 06:07:31.110
every time we create a todo
record in the database,

06:07:31.110 --> 06:07:34.190
the database will automatically
add a default value

06:07:34.190 --> 06:07:37.010
to our UID, and we will provide

06:07:37.010 --> 06:07:38.770
the created at, text, and done.

06:07:42.662 --> 06:07:46.380
Okay, that was a bit of
configuration here, now,

06:07:46.380 --> 06:07:49.750
the error we received in
the console before was that

06:07:49.750 --> 06:07:53.820
this file didn't exist, so
we couldn't start the server.

06:07:54.710 --> 06:07:57.830
Now there's one more thing we need to do.

06:07:59.210 --> 06:08:03.040
Remember in the Gitpod YAML
we specified the DATABASE_URL

06:08:04.350 --> 06:08:06.530
as Postgres localhost blah blah?

06:08:07.530 --> 06:08:09.240
If we were to commit these changes

06:08:09.240 --> 06:08:12.430
and start a new Gitpod workspace,
that would be available,

06:08:12.430 --> 06:08:17.280
but at the moment, if
we do echo DATABASE_URL,

06:08:19.190 --> 06:08:22.530
we actually, we have it because
I think I already ran it,

06:08:22.530 --> 06:08:26.155
but that, we have to make
sure this environment

06:08:26.155 --> 06:08:28.560
where it was to set, so we do have it,

06:08:28.560 --> 06:08:33.560
and now let's try to clear
this and run npx prisma studio.

06:08:35.560 --> 06:08:36.570
Give that a second.

06:08:38.100 --> 06:08:43.100
It is saying that services
available in port 5555.

06:08:43.560 --> 06:08:46.820
We can close that preview
here, but if we open a browser,

06:08:47.970 --> 06:08:52.970
it opens up the Prisma
Studio and you can see that

06:08:53.040 --> 06:08:55.320
it shows us all the models we defined,

06:08:55.320 --> 06:08:57.690
so in this case Todo model,

06:08:57.690 --> 06:09:02.690
that's the only one we defined
in our schema down here.

06:09:04.220 --> 06:09:05.810
So if we click on that,

06:09:07.633 --> 06:09:10.020
it gives an error, unable to run script.

06:09:11.090 --> 06:09:13.950
Let's have a look invalidate, blah, blah,

06:09:13.950 --> 06:09:15.850
and then here the table

06:09:15.850 --> 06:09:19.180
public todo does not exist
in the current database.

06:09:20.050 --> 06:09:25.050
That is true because we never
actually created that table.

06:09:26.450 --> 06:09:30.470
The only thing we did
was we defined the model,

06:09:30.470 --> 06:09:33.190
but we never told Prisma

06:09:33.190 --> 06:09:36.460
to create that table in the database.

06:09:37.460 --> 06:09:42.460
We could go to the database
and manually run a SQL query

06:09:42.490 --> 06:09:45.880
that, you know, create
table with these fields.

06:09:45.880 --> 06:09:47.810
But the whole point of using Prisma

06:09:47.810 --> 06:09:49.880
is that we can automate all that.

06:09:49.880 --> 06:09:54.850
So let's stop our studio with control C

06:09:55.720 --> 06:09:58.720
and I will show you how we can ensure

06:09:58.720 --> 06:10:02.730
that the database is always up to date.

06:10:02.730 --> 06:10:07.220
So the command we can
use to make this work is

06:10:08.800 --> 06:10:13.543
again, we use npx prisma migrate dev

06:10:15.690 --> 06:10:19.340
let's see what happens, basically migrate,

06:10:21.220 --> 06:10:23.580
is going to look at the Prisma file

06:10:23.580 --> 06:10:27.440
it's going to see that
there's a todo record.

06:10:28.660 --> 06:10:31.980
And when it sees that record,

06:10:31.980 --> 06:10:34.950
it will then go to the
database and check whether

06:10:34.950 --> 06:10:38.580
the database matches what's
configured in our schema.

06:10:38.580 --> 06:10:41.090
And let's see what happens
at the moment, just FYI,

06:10:41.090 --> 06:10:43.360
the database is empty.

06:10:43.360 --> 06:10:47.050
So there's going to be a
discrepancy between what we expect

06:10:47.050 --> 06:10:49.820
the database to look
like with the todo table

06:10:51.333 --> 06:10:53.980
and the empty environment in the database,

06:10:53.980 --> 06:10:55.130
let's run that command.

06:10:59.900 --> 06:11:04.280
Alright, so let's walk
through that together.

06:11:04.280 --> 06:11:08.230
Prisma schema loaded from
this file, excellent.

06:11:08.230 --> 06:11:11.590
Datasource db is Postgres, mhm very good.

06:11:12.790 --> 06:11:16.560
Schemas, public and localhosts, 5432,

06:11:16.560 --> 06:11:18.980
that looks all good.

