there are now two terminals.

06:29:09.920 --> 06:29:12.510
The second one that's
currently highlighted

06:29:12.510 --> 06:29:13.850
is the Prisma Studio.

06:29:14.790 --> 06:29:17.600
And the first one is the one

06:29:17.600 --> 06:29:21.170
that runs our web
application on port 3000.

06:29:21.170 --> 06:29:23.580
So you can navigate between the two

06:29:23.580 --> 06:29:27.330
if you want to start or
stop any of the services.

06:29:29.080 --> 06:29:33.060
Okay so the main file we're
going to work with today

06:29:33.935 --> 06:29:36.610
is source, routes, todos, _api.

06:29:37.500 --> 06:29:42.500
Now we moved all that code
into this file a while ago,

06:29:43.370 --> 06:29:47.770
and now we're going to actually
see some benefits of that

06:29:47.770 --> 06:29:50.830
because any interactions we've
had with that todos array

06:29:50.830 --> 06:29:54.970
is now consolidated into that one file.

06:29:54.970 --> 06:29:56.750
So now that we're going to replace that

06:29:56.750 --> 06:29:59.340
with the database table we created.

06:29:59.340 --> 06:30:01.690
We can just update to code below here

06:30:02.770 --> 06:30:05.930
to leverage calls to the database.

06:30:07.490 --> 06:30:12.490
So when we ran the migrate dev command

06:30:13.760 --> 06:30:18.760
from Prisma, so in the
package.json our predev script,

06:30:18.950 --> 06:30:23.950
prisma migrate dev, that
generated our Prisma Client.

06:30:24.260 --> 06:30:29.260
If you recall, in the terminal,
if we scroll up just a bit,

06:30:29.750 --> 06:30:31.900
it generated the,

06:30:31.900 --> 06:30:34.560
it used the migration and
applied it to the database

06:30:34.560 --> 06:30:38.460
because we started a new
workspace, the database was empty,

06:30:38.460 --> 06:30:39.370
and it also generated

06:30:39.370 --> 06:30:44.370
that Prisma client into
node_modules @prisma/client.

06:30:44.730 --> 06:30:48.090
Okay, now that client,

06:30:48.090 --> 06:30:49.780
we will see that in a second

06:30:49.780 --> 06:30:52.350
is fully aware of our database model.

06:30:52.350 --> 06:30:57.060
And we can import that because
it's in the node_modules

06:30:57.060 --> 06:31:01.080
directory, we can input it like
we would any other package.

06:31:01.080 --> 06:31:06.080
So at the root of our
file here at the very top,

06:31:06.190 --> 06:31:09.850
we can add a new line saying import

06:31:13.360 --> 06:31:17.870
Prisma from @prisma/client.

06:31:19.160 --> 06:31:23.320
And we can then use that
instead of, you know,

06:31:23.320 --> 06:31:25.820
todo.push, for example,

06:31:25.820 --> 06:31:30.300
we can use that new Prisma import.

06:31:30.300 --> 06:31:32.920
Now there is one gotcha.

06:31:32.920 --> 06:31:37.920
and that is related to
a temporary workaround

06:31:39.360 --> 06:31:44.360
we need to implement until
everybody moves to ES modules.

06:31:44.500 --> 06:31:49.330
So that line import
Prisma from Prisma client

06:31:50.873 --> 06:31:55.357
works well in our environment,
but once we deploy to Vercel,

06:31:56.540 --> 06:31:59.150
we'll need to import
this slightly different.

06:32:00.280 --> 06:32:02.890
Long story, there's
some information online,

06:32:02.890 --> 06:32:05.670
but we basically want to extract that

06:32:05.670 --> 06:32:10.150
for the time being into
separate file and allow us

06:32:10.150 --> 06:32:13.300
to deal with that workaround.

06:32:13.300 --> 06:32:16.630
So just bear with me as
we go to source, lib,

06:32:16.630 --> 06:32:20.297
and we create a new file
here called prisma.ts.

06:32:23.550 --> 06:32:24.790
And within that,

06:32:24.790 --> 06:32:28.170
we basically implement
the loading mechanism

06:32:28.170 --> 06:32:30.740
of both approaches,

06:32:30.740 --> 06:32:32.640
one that works on our
development environment

06:32:32.640 --> 06:32:36.830
and one that works on
Vercel, and then we will use

06:32:37.715 --> 06:32:41.281
that in our codes, in the API
file so that we don't have

06:32:41.281 --> 06:32:44.650
to deal with any if else
statements and whatnot over there.

06:32:44.650 --> 06:32:49.250
So the way we do that is we import Prisma,

06:32:49.250 --> 06:32:53.193
but also we import star as PrismaAll

06:32:56.810 --> 06:32:58.960
from the Prisma Client.

06:32:59.820 --> 06:33:00.720
So as I mentioned,

06:33:01.950 --> 06:33:05.210
depending on whether we
run on our environment here

06:33:05.210 --> 06:33:07.420
or in Vercel, this one,

06:33:07.420 --> 06:33:10.740
or this one will be
undefined, one of the two,

06:33:10.740 --> 06:33:11.810
and it's the opposite way,

06:33:11.810 --> 06:33:13.540
depending on the environment we're on.

06:33:13.540 --> 06:33:18.540
So all we need to do now is we
say the actual Prisma Client

06:33:18.710 --> 06:33:21.970
is either Prisma if it's available,

06:33:21.970 --> 06:33:26.970
so TypeScript, we can use question mark.

06:33:26.980 --> 06:33:31.980
So Prisma Client or alternatively,

06:33:34.104 --> 06:33:38.620
PrismaAll again, if it's
available, dot PrismaClient.

06:33:38.620 --> 06:33:43.620
And then we see export
default, the PrismaClient.

06:33:46.010 --> 06:33:49.550
And now with that, we don't
have to worry anywhere else

06:33:49.550 --> 06:33:53.970
in the code, whether this
approach loaded the correct client

06:33:53.970 --> 06:33:58.110
or this approach did with
the star as Prisma Client,

06:33:58.110 --> 06:34:00.660
all because in this line here,

06:34:00.660 --> 06:34:03.610
we basically just pick
either this or the other one

06:34:03.610 --> 06:34:05.420
and export to Prisma Client.

06:34:07.111 --> 06:34:09.646
With that in our API,

06:34:09.646 --> 06:34:14.646
we can simply say import the PrismaClient

06:34:17.670 --> 06:34:22.190
from remember the
shortcut with $lib/prisma.

06:34:23.510 --> 06:34:27.500
And now we can use that Prisma
client just like we would

06:34:27.500 --> 06:34:31.760
if we imported it regularly
according to the Prisma docs.

06:34:31.760 --> 06:34:36.280
A small little workaround
that we need to make this work

06:34:36.280 --> 06:34:39.610
on the local environment
or in Gitpod here,

06:34:39.610 --> 06:34:40.790
as well as on Vercel.

06:34:41.960 --> 06:34:44.540
Now, the probably most exciting part

06:34:44.540 --> 06:34:46.950
of this entire course is that

06:34:46.950 --> 06:34:50.567
we can finally get rid of this todos array

06:34:50.567 --> 06:34:54.930
and no longer the todo items in memory

06:34:54.930 --> 06:34:58.090
and lose them every time
we restart the server.

06:34:58.090 --> 06:35:03.090
So let's celebrate by dropping
this, hooray, is gone,

06:35:03.760 --> 06:35:05.980
we'll never have to worry about it again.

06:35:05.980 --> 06:35:08.374
Instead what are we going to do

06:35:08.374 --> 06:35:11.800
is we create a new instance
of that PrismaClient.

06:35:11.800 --> 06:35:15.370
Let's call it, Prisma
equals new PrismaClient.

06:35:17.490 --> 06:35:20.590
And now you'll see the power
of Prisma in just a second,

06:35:20.590 --> 06:35:23.050
if we take this and

06:35:25.427 --> 06:35:27.680
we start to go through all the places

06:35:27.680 --> 06:35:30.900
where we access currently
that array, and we replace it

06:35:30.900 --> 06:35:33.990
with a call to the database,
let's check this out.

06:35:33.990 --> 06:35:38.990
So here we want to
fetch all the todo-items

06:35:39.810 --> 06:35:42.870
that are persisted in the
database, so let's look at this.

06:35:42.870 --> 06:35:47.870
We use Prisma dot and now the autocomplete

06:35:50.950 --> 06:35:53.320
show us a todo property

06:35:57.020 --> 06:36:00.927
with that todo, we can then say find many.

06:36:02.870 --> 06:36:06.390
And you can see there's code
examples right in the docs.

06:36:06.390 --> 06:36:11.390
So that get all todos is
const todos equals await

06:36:11.950 --> 06:36:13.760
Prisma todo findMany.

06:36:13.760 --> 06:36:15.530
So we'll do findMany.

06:36:15.530 --> 06:36:20.530
We call that and this returns a promise.

06:36:21.240 --> 06:36:24.590
So we'll say await and we
make this an async function.

06:36:27.350 --> 06:36:28.590
Unbelievably cool.

06:36:29.500 --> 06:36:32.080
You can tell I'm really
excited about this.

06:36:32.080 --> 06:36:37.070
This is such a game changer
to how we've developed with

06:36:37.070 --> 06:36:40.270
or interacted with database
from JavaScript in the last 20

06:36:40.270 --> 06:36:45.270
years, Prisma really sets the
bar high with that approach.

06:36:45.630 --> 06:36:48.400
That's literally all we
need to do, and as you saw,

06:36:48.400 --> 06:36:53.400
we had full TypeScript
support for our todo-item.

06:36:53.590 --> 06:36:57.970
If we were to go into the Prisma schema

06:36:57.970 --> 06:37:01.720
and start to add more
models or update the model,

06:37:01.720 --> 06:37:04.240
every time we start a dev server,

06:37:04.240 --> 06:37:07.440
the new client will be generated
and we would have access

06:37:07.440 --> 06:37:10.440
to these new fields and functions
and everything immediately

06:37:11.460 --> 06:37:13.430
through TypeScript autocomplete here.

06:37:14.340 --> 06:37:15.173
Okay.

06:37:16.690 --> 06:37:21.690
The next thing is we want
to create a new todo-item.

06:37:22.040 --> 06:37:25.560
So far we just set todos.push,

06:37:25.560 --> 06:37:29.310
now, this is no longer the way to do it.

06:37:29.310 --> 06:37:34.310
The new way to do it is that
we say await prisma todo,

06:37:40.020 --> 06:37:44.540
and guess what, create, ah, so cool.

06:37:45.570 --> 06:37:50.320
And within that, we have autocomplete,

06:37:50.320 --> 06:37:54.420
which asks us to provide a data property.

06:37:55.400 --> 06:37:58.100
We will happily do that, and as part

06:37:58.100 --> 06:38:01.800
of that data property, you
can now see that it's asking

06:38:02.663 --> 06:38:06.560
for created at, done,
text and an optional UID.

06:38:06.560 --> 06:38:09.610
Again, fully autogenerated
because of our model.

06:38:10.730 --> 06:38:15.730
And we know what these values
are, so let's go ahead.

06:38:17.140 --> 06:38:22.140
Top to bottom, created at
is the data dot created,

06:38:23.640 --> 06:38:27.160
oops created, what did
we call it created at?

06:38:28.080 --> 06:38:31.700
And we know this is a date,
so we'll tell TypeScript that.

06:38:33.090 --> 06:38:35.910
And then we have done,
which comes from data.done,

06:38:38.880 --> 06:38:40.230
that's a Boolean.

06:38:42.510 --> 06:38:43.750
All right.

06:38:43.750 --> 06:38:48.750
And we have the text, which
is data.text as string.

06:38:51.100 --> 06:38:52.150
And with that,

06:38:52.150 --> 06:38:56.520
we now have a new item
created in the database.

06:38:56.520 --> 06:38:59.780
So that push is no longer needed.

06:39:01.230 --> 06:39:04.000
Now here's one thing we have to change.

06:39:04.000 --> 06:39:07.100
And the reason we need to change here is

06:39:08.690 --> 06:39:13.483
so far our post endpoint
added to UID right here.

06:39:14.340 --> 06:39:16.210
This is no longer the case,

06:39:16.210 --> 06:39:18.820
but when we add an item to the database,

06:39:18.820 --> 06:39:23.820
the response to the frontend
has to include the UID,

06:39:23.980 --> 06:39:27.460
or basically it has to
include the todo-item

06:39:27.460 --> 06:39:31.230
with the same data that
we have in the database.

06:39:31.230 --> 06:39:33.470
So we could, you know,

06:39:33.470 --> 06:39:38.040
use Prisma to go fetch the
newly created item somehow.

06:39:38.040 --> 06:39:41.682
But the better way is
that the create function

06:39:41.682 --> 06:39:46.430
actually returns the created item for us.

06:39:46.430 --> 06:39:51.430
So we can just say, body
equals and drop this part here.

06:39:53.530 --> 06:39:56.477
Now we create the item in the database

06:39:56.477 --> 06:39:58.380
and when that's complete,

06:39:58.380 --> 06:40:00.160
the response we get back from the database

06:40:00.160 --> 06:40:03.230
is newly created item
that contains the UID.

06:40:06.116 --> 06:40:08.060
Moving on, let's see how
we can delete an item.

06:40:09.160 --> 06:40:12.610
This doesn't exist, but we can say

06:40:16.810 --> 06:40:21.810
await Prisma.todo dot,

06:40:24.030 --> 06:40:25.250
let's see, do we have a delete?

06:40:25.250 --> 06:40:26.650
We have a delete right here.

06:40:27.630 --> 06:40:29.290
And what do we want to do?

06:40:30.560 --> 06:40:34.630
In the docs, It says that
it wants a where clause,

06:40:34.630 --> 06:40:39.020
where we can specify which
element we want to delete.

06:40:39.020 --> 06:40:40.680
All right, let's have a look.

06:40:40.680 --> 06:40:44.510
We'll pass in an object
with a where clause.

06:40:45.930 --> 06:40:48.720
And it's suggesting that we provide a UID.

06:40:49.670 --> 06:40:51.040
Let's do that.

06:40:51.040 --> 06:40:53.377
We know that the UID we want to delete

06:40:53.377 --> 06:40:57.600
is the one we get from
the request params.UID.

06:40:59.040 --> 06:41:04.040
And that will take care
of removing that element.

06:41:06.500 --> 06:41:10.960
We can apply whatever response
we get back to the body

06:41:10.960 --> 06:41:11.793
if we wanted to.

06:41:12.660 --> 06:41:14.190
That's all good.

06:41:14.190 --> 06:41:18.120
Lastly let's look at how we can patch

06:41:18.120 --> 06:41:21.210
an object in the database.

06:41:21.210 --> 06:41:23.100
So none of that is needed anymore.

06:41:25.590 --> 06:41:30.560
As well as the body, we
will do that in one line,

06:41:32.030 --> 06:41:37.030
body equals await prisma todo

06:41:37.480 --> 06:41:40.210
and then guess what,
there's an update function.

06:41:41.120 --> 06:41:44.880
And the update function wants an object,

06:41:44.880 --> 06:41:47.220
let's have a look data and where,

06:41:47.220 --> 06:41:49.640
okay, we are already
familiar with the where.

06:41:49.640 --> 06:41:51.660
So we can just use that.

06:41:51.660 --> 06:41:55.130
We know it wants a UID, which we have.

06:41:56.040 --> 06:41:59.850
And also it wants a data property,

06:41:59.850 --> 06:42:03.500
which presumably is going
to be the data we want

06:42:03.500 --> 06:42:07.530
to update for the given
todo-item we selected up here.

06:42:08.510 --> 06:42:13.270
So data, we have data,
text, and todo done.

06:42:13.270 --> 06:42:18.270
So let's say done, and the
value for that is data.done.

06:42:19.830 --> 06:42:24.830
And the other thing is the text.

06:42:26.670 --> 06:42:31.670
And that is data.text
that we want to put there.

06:42:33.610 --> 06:42:38.610
Okay, now we don't need
that code from before,

06:42:40.963 --> 06:42:41.796
we don't need that.

06:42:42.650 --> 06:42:44.140
That looks good.

06:42:44.140 --> 06:42:49.140
And what we can do now is go have a look

06:42:49.200 --> 06:42:53.590
at what this looks like
in the user interface.

06:42:53.590 --> 06:42:56.660
So first of all, let's go all
the way to the bottom here.

06:42:58.790 --> 06:43:00.630
This looks good, seems to compile.

06:43:01.540 --> 06:43:04.200
If we switch over to the application,

06:43:04.200 --> 06:43:08.330
let's inspect the network tab.

06:43:09.250 --> 06:43:14.250
So here we'll add a first item,
that seemed to have worked,

06:43:14.800 --> 06:43:17.890
something happened, at
least we show it in the UI.

06:43:17.890 --> 06:43:22.280
Let's look at the requests.

06:43:25.670 --> 06:43:28.410
Todos JSON, all right.

06:43:28.410 --> 06:43:33.410
We sent the text, and in
return, we got an item.

06:43:33.810 --> 06:43:36.960
There's our new UID
generated by the database.

06:43:36.960 --> 06:43:39.350
Definitely a lot more unique

06:43:39.350 --> 06:43:41.840
than our timestamp that we had before.

06:43:41.840 --> 06:43:46.290
Again, a reminder, do not use
Date.now as a UID, never ever.

06:43:49.000 --> 06:43:53.350
So very cool, let's look at
Prisma Studio to double check

06:43:53.350 --> 06:43:56.180
if that item actually got persisted.

06:43:56.180 --> 06:43:58.890
So heading over to the Prisma Studio tab,

06:43:58.890 --> 06:44:03.070
and there's a little refresh icon, voila,

06:44:03.070 --> 06:44:08.070
